window.harnessChunks["rows-91ead4e9c9a96aa2.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-18T23:12:56+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/lobehub/lobehub\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">LobeHub</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-18\">2026-09-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/lobehub/lobehub/tree/3af256caae11796da471bec5f57d882853f30f19\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3af256caae11796da471bec5f57d882853f30f19\">3af256c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: specialized agents autonomously perform assigned conversational, research, creation or tool-using work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: open-ended user tasks, domain-specific information, tool/model observations and task-specific uncertainty. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the substantive actions and output needed for the agent&#39;s assigned task or conversational role. - Decision owner: the invoked model-driven agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agent configuration, system role, model/provider selection, plugins, isolated async task context and session state. - Closure path: agent receives an instruction, performs work, returns a result to the group/user, and that result becomes input to subsequent orchestration or conversation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model-driven agent owns substantive task execution while LobeHub transports context/tools/results. - Evidence: README.md, docs/usage/agent/agent-team.mdx, agent-management manifest/system role. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: coordinate multiple specialized S1 agents by choosing dependent versus parallel work, agent selection, handoff order and group interaction mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: dependency-sensitive work that would be wrong in parallel, redundant/overlapping contributions, poor handoffs and unstructured multi-agent interaction. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose whether to use one agent, broadcast, sequential speaking, one async task or multiple parallel tasks, and choose the participating agents/instructions. - Decision owner: the model-driven Group Supervisor / Orchestrator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: speak, broadcast, executeAgentTask, executeAgentTasks, group orchestration runtime, shared group context and supervisor resume after member completion. - Closure path: supervisor analyzes the request, selects a coordination pattern and dispatches agents; member results return to the supervisor, which synthesizes, follows up or changes the next interaction. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic runtime executes the selected pattern, but the supervisor model owns the coordination discretion over mode, agent selection and follow-up. - Evidence: packages/builtin-tool-group-management/src/systemRole.ts, docs/usage/agent/agent-team.mdx, group orchestration runtime. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: generic callAgent alone would not establish S2; the positive mapping depends on the explicit dependency/parallelism decision framework and returned supervisor loop. - Distinct S1 units: two or more specialized agents in an Agent Group, each capable of separate operational work. - Inter-S1 disturbance: dependent subtasks can be run in the wrong order, overlapping agents can add redundant work, and independent subtasks can be unnecessarily serialized; first-party guidance also calls out weak handoffs and overlapping roles as collaboration failures. - Attenuating coordination relation: the Group Supervisor chooses sequential, parallel, broadcast or focused modes and assigns work to agents according to dependency and expertise. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: member outputs resume the supervisor; it can ask follow-ups, synthesize, invoke another agent or terminate the group turn, thereby changing later contributions. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: first-party supervisor policy explicitly diagnoses dependence/parallelizability and selects an attenuation pattern for interactions among distinct operational agents, with returned member results driving further coordination.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no separate whole-group current-control function over resources, commitments, priorities or constraints is established beyond the S2 orchestration itself. - Disturbance / variety regulated: group task routing, model choice and current conversation state were inspected, but no distinct S3 current-control loop is established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no material first-party owner was found with a whole-system current view and separate authority over group-wide commitments/resources/accountability. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agent management, group profile editing, async task execution, scheduling and supervisor orchestration. - Closure path: no qualifying distinct S3 loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the orchestrator&#39;s evidenced rights are inter-agent task/mode coordination for the current group operation; naming it supervisor/operator is not enough to promote that same function to S3. - Evidence: group-management system role, Agent Group docs, agent-management tools. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: review another agent&#39;s produced work through a distinct reviewer/editor role and return critique into revision/re-review. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete, unclear, inconsistent or low-quality outputs that require a second perspective before acceptance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge produced work, issue feedback and require/refine another contribution. - Decision owner: constructor path; LobeHub supplies explicit iterative/reviewer group patterns, but the developer/user must compose the reviewer role, independence and authority in the concrete group. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: iterative Agent Group mode, distinct agent identities, sequential handoffs, shared context and supervisor-mediated repeated turns. - Closure path: producer emits work; reviewer/editor returns critique; producer revises; reviewer can perform final review in the documented iterative pattern. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a separate model-driven reviewer can own the judgment once composed, but the standard distribution does not assign an invariant independent reviewer or audit authority automatically. - Evidence: docs/usage/agent/agent-team.mdx, packages/builtin-tool-group-management/src/systemRole.ts. - Basis: explicit - Confidence: medium - Caveats: ordinary debate or synthesis is not counted as S3; the positive constructor claim is limited to the explicitly documented review/refine pattern with distinct roles. - Claim being audited: that another agent&#39;s draft/output is sufficiently correct, clear or complete for the group&#39;s purpose. - Ordinary reporting path: producer agent returns its draft/result through the normal group turn. - Complementary access path: a separately configured Editor/reviewer/QA agent receives the produced work and can critique it in an iterative group workflow. - Independence boundary: first-party group mechanics preserve distinct agent identities, but organizational independence/authority of the reviewer is supplied by the group composition rather than guaranteed by runtime policy. - Who acts on findings: the producing agent receives reviewer feedback and revises; the group/supervisor can route the revised result back for final review.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no separate external/prospective organizational intelligence loop is established at the Agent Group boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user preference/history changes are learned, but no distinct future/environment adaptation function is closed. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no material first-party owner was found that senses external/future distinctions, generates adaptation options and returns a selected change into group capability/S3. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Personal Memory, editable structured memory, continual learning and tool/search capability. - Closure path: memory changes later agent context directly; no separate outside-and-then option-development loop is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: personalization/learning and external tools do not automatically establish S4. - Evidence: README.md, Agent Group/runtime docs. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity/ultimate-policy governance function is established for the Agent Group. - Disturbance / variety regulated: group prompt, member roster and agent configuration can change, but no ultimate identity dispute/authority loop is evidenced. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no material first-party identity-level authoritative decision with a return-to-operation closure was found. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: group profile, Agent Builder, agent CRUD, prompts, skills and user configuration. - Closure path: configuration edits alter operation, but they are not an evidenced S5 identity/ultimate-policy process. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: prompts and editable configuration are implementation/control artifacts, not proof of VSM S5. - Evidence: docs/usage/agent/agent-team.mdx, agent-management manifest/system role. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9f83e41179680c24cbe23a5091abda11fcc221d0/assessments/lobehub.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9f83e41179680c24cbe23a5091abda11fcc221d0\">9f83e41 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-12T16:19:31+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/kyegomez/swarms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Swarms</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-12\">2026-10-12</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/kyegomez/swarms/tree/3009364a7e44dc3e55fec238b56b3a4fa6949a05\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3009364a7e44dc3e55fec238b56b3a4fa6949a05\">3009364 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-directed individual first-party Agent solves a task via model/tool choice, observation and iterative continuation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Dynamic goals, changing tool outcomes, external model output, context and task errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose steps, call tools/subagents and decide when to continue or finish an operating task. - Decision owner: Per-agent hosted LLM/Agent runtime, not merely an agent-shaped tuple invoked by a deterministic sequencer. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent run, runautonomousloop, tool executor, model wrapper and workspace. - Closure path: Task → model-generated action/tool use → environment result → next model reasoning and eventual completion. - Boundary reachability: Python Agent.run and optional autonomous-loop configuration are used from the published distribution (README.md; swarms/structs/agent.py). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Context-sensitive model actions select operating acts; loop counters and provider wrappers do not own that choice. - Evidence: swarms/structs/agent.py; README.md. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: In bounded GroupChat mode, mediate multiple model-agent bids competing for a single shared conversational speaking slot, preventing speaker domination while retaining useful contributions. - Distinct S1 units: A configured GroupChat.agents set of independently invoked Agent objects, each producing a context-contingent (score, reply) via model-backed respond tool. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Several operating agents seek the same shared conversation floor simultaneously; unchecked publication produces competing turns, and repeated high-frequency speakers dominate the shared information channel. - Attenuating coordination relation: collectbids calls each agent, selectspeaker applies a shared threshold and recency penalty, chooses exactly one speaker, and post updates common conversation history seen by all peers on the next turn. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: The selected speaker&#39;s contribution, not all conflicting bids, enters shared history for every agent&#39;s following model turn; recent speakers are penalized in future selections. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The explicit gate is driven by simultaneous claims to a scarce common conversational medium and recurring dominance, with a fairness attenuation policy and returned altered information environment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicting simultaneous bids, message crowding and disproportionate conversational control. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select exactly one admissible turn among competing agent contributions. - Decision owner: First-party deterministic GroupChat floor arbiter, therefore C, not a separate agentic S2 decision owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: respond(score,message) tool, threshold, recency window/penalty, chosen-speaker posting and shared Conversation. - Closure path: Independently formed bids → contention/fairness gate → one allowed published contribution → next turn&#39;s changed conversation and adjusted future bids. - Boundary reachability: GroupChat.run uses the packaged bidding, selecting and posting operations; this is an opt-in published architecture, not an inactive helper (swarms/structs/groupchat.py; README.md). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agents determine proposed content and scores; the decisive inter-agent floor grant is deterministic host policy (C). - Evidence: swarms/structs/groupchat.py; swarms/structs/conversation.py; README.md. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Applies to GroupChat&#39;s shared conversation medium, not arbitrary shared file changes or all Swarms topologies. Does not claim broad resource conflict arbitration.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Model-led HierarchicalSwarm director assigns present worker orders and adjusts ongoing team work from collected subordinate results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Divergent worker progress/results, uncertain task division, errors and changing completion quality. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Produce a model-derived order batch, choose which specialist performs what, inspect returned outputs and issue fresh orders in further loops. - Decision owner: First-party configured model director; deterministic parseorders and executeorders relay its decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: HierarchicalSwarm director, OrderBatch parser, worker dispatch and optional feedback director, conversation storage. - Whole-system current view: Director sees the bounded swarm conversation/worker results and prior execution history, not every organization using Swarms. - Current-control decision scope: Current worker allocation, ordering, revisions and reattempts for the HierarchicalSwarm instance. - Closure path: Director plans and orders → specialized workers run → results return and enter conversation → director next loop reassigns/replans. - Boundary reachability: HierarchicalSwarm.run calls step, rundirector, parseorders, executeorders, and loops over result feedback when maxloops allows (swarms/structs/hiearchicalswarm.py). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Actual work orders originate in a model-led director within the shipped topology; loop counters and dispatcher enforce, not replace, task choices. - Evidence: swarms/structs/hiearchicalswarm.py; README.md. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: ? - Function: Some prebuilt modes include judge/verification or consensus phases; materially independent operating evidence inspection and correction path remains unresolved. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Invalid subordinate claims, low-quality task completion or conflicting agent assertions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Judge can evaluate outputs; whether it has independent primary-source evidence and authority over correction is not guaranteed. - Decision owner: Configured judge/consensus agent, not established as a truly separately observing auditor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Hierarchical judge toggle, MajorityVoting consensus and HeavySwarm verification-oriented roles. - Claim being audited: Specialist worker answer/output claims. - Ordinary reporting path: Worker results added to director conversation, majority-voting conversation or final report. - Complementary access path: Shipped judging paths chiefly pass worker outputs/derived conversation text; a separate direct observation of work artifacts has not been established at the first-party boundary. - Independence boundary: A different model/persona is not a guaranteed independent observations channel. - Who acts on findings: Directors or consensus can summarize and reconsider, but an independent audit finding → corrective operational reroute is unproven. - Closure path: Output → judge/consensus verdict → report or next loop, insufficient to classify a distinct S3 audit under the governing method. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Another LLM rating peer output is not equivalent to a complementary function sampling reality independently. - Evidence: swarms/structs/hiearchicalswarm.py; swarms/structs/majorityvoting.py; swarms/structs/heavyswarm.py. - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: ? - Function: Planning prompts, persistent workspace and memory may help later tasks but a decision owner adapting future systemic capability from external prospective observations has not been established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future requirements, market or environment shifts and changing task complexity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Unresolved among user-supplied swarm config, model director and persisted task memory. - Decision owner: No uniquely identified first-party outside-and-then intelligence controller at the bounded swarm recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Conversation/workspace storage, planning role prompts, dynamic agent layouts. - External distinction: Outside facts can enter model context, but information access alone is not S4. - Future / prospective distinction: Multi-loop plans address current task commitments, not shown to adapt future organization capability. - Adaptation option generated: No verified capability-evolution choice produced by a distinct owner. - Path back into current capability / S3: Persistence and plan prompts can affect later runs, but external foresight → applied organizational redesign remains unresolved. - Closure path: Retained context → later work, incomplete future-intelligence and capability-change loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Prompted planning and memory are not enough for S4. - Evidence: swarms/structs/hiearchicalswarm.py; swarms/utils/workspacemanager.py; swarms/structs/agent.py. - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: ? - Function: Configured swarm identity, roles and execution limits exist but an ultimate organizational purpose/policy closure is not traced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User boundaries, acceptable agent/tool behavior and system-level authority. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Developer/user chooses prompts, members and parameters; no proven ultimate-policy choice by a qualified first-party actor. - Decision owner: User-supplied configuration for role identity and deterministic safety checks; a complete parent policy mode remains unresolved. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: system prompts, task role configuration and tool/platform restrictions. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Who chooses and revises the organization&#39;s overarching identity, purpose and final authority. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: Not established beyond out-of-band user/developer configuration. - Return-to-operation path: Prompt and parameter selection guides run behavior, but no complete governing policy feedback is proven. - Closure path: Configured goals/roles → worker execution; no verified policy challenge/ultimate revision returned to operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A system prompt or fixed role roster is not itself an ultimate decision loop. - Evidence: swarms/structs/hiearchicalswarm.py; swarms/structs/groupchat.py; swarms/structs/agent.py. - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/5b1d5a9c5a355aedd4320e3898fd3b2af3f52800/assessments/swarms.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 5b1d5a9c5a355aedd4320e3898fd3b2af3f52800\">5b1d5a9 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T21:31:18+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/camel-ai/camel\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CAMEL Workforce</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-17\">2026-09-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/camel-ai/camel/tree/8c791b7b9cf7deab56cb5a92818c34499af9097f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 8c791b7b9cf7deab56cb5a92818c34499af9097f\">8c791b7 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: A. Worker agents autonomously execute assigned task work through model/tool loops.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: C. Workforce maintains explicit cross-task dependency state and gates release/order of work across multiple workers. This prevents dependent S1 work from proceeding against unsatisfied predecessor commitments, establishing a concrete coordination path beyond routing. The mechanism is supplied deterministically by Workforce while the application supplies the task graph, so ownership is constructor-side rather than agent-owned.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: A. The Coordinator operates over shared workforce state, assigns work based on worker capability, tracks current commitments, handles failures, and can create new workers or redirect/replan work. These are whole-workforce current-control rights over assignments and available operational capacity. The model-driven coordinator owns the runtime choice within configured bounds.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: —. Quality/failure analysis evaluates returned task results in the ordinary Workforce control/reporting path. No materially complementary evidence-access channel or protected independent audit boundary was established. The historical C therefore does not survive current S3 criteria.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: —. Replanning and dynamic worker creation respond to current-task failure; they do not establish an external/future environmental intelligence loop that changes organizational capability.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: —. Human pause/stop/skip controls and the externally supplied objective are parent/operator controls over ordinary execution, not a demonstrated ultimate-policy/identity closure path. The historical P is therefore removed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/cfe27303e117e1fd3a79fc8f485f33caada591a4/assessments/camel-workforce.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit cfe27303e117e1fd3a79fc8f485f33caada591a4\">cfe2730 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-19T19:14:57+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/micro/go-micro\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Go Micro</a><small>2015</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-19\">2026-09-19</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/micro/go-micro/tree/792b18c3010715e2f12621f0120bf058be225482\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 792b18c3010715e2f12621f0120bf058be225482\">792b18c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously transform user/task state through a model → tool/service/delegation → observation loop until an operational result is produced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user goals, changing service/tool availability, tool/RPC/A2A results, persistent conversation state, provider responses, tool failures and intermediate observations requiring context-sensitive next actions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next service/custom/built-in tool, decide whether to plan or delegate, consume returned observations, continue the loop or settle the answer. - Decision owner: the active Go Micro LLM agent in NewAgent or micro chat direct-service mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model adapters, service registry/tool discovery, built-in plan/delegate/request-input tools, memory/store, tool wrappers, step/loop/spend limits, approvals, checkpointing, retry/timeout logic, RPC/A2A/MCP integration. - Closure path: user/assigned task + current context → agent selects action/tool/delegation → Go Micro executes/gates it → tool/service/sub-agent result returns into model context → agent selects another action or final result. - Boundary reachability: NewAgent and micro chat are shipped, documented first-party runtime modes; the positive S1 loop is implemented in agent/ and CLI runtime code rather than inferred from examples or repository dogfood. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model-driven agent while retaining registry, memory, tool execution and guardrails leaves no contextual actor choosing the next operational action. Deterministic wrappers constrain and transport the model&#39;s decisions rather than replacing them. - Evidence: README.md, agent/builtin.go, cmd/micro/chat/chat.go. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: external model inference and remote A2A peers are substrate/environment. The positive state is established independently by the first-party agent loop around those dependencies.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2-specific loop was established that regulates a concrete interference/conflict/oscillation among distinct Go Micro S1 agent units at the declared product-runtime recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate paths inspected included multiple registered agents, RPC/A2A handoffs, service discovery/load balancing, durable flows, duplicate delegate/retry protection and shared registry/store infrastructure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying S2-specific decision right is supplied. Delegation selects another actor for a subtask; service discovery/load balancing routes calls; idempotency/retry controls duplicate side effects; flow edges order work. - Decision owner: none established for S2 in the reviewed standard product-runtime boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: registry, client load balancing, broker/store, plan/delegate tools, A2A/MCP transport, flow sequencing, retry/idempotency guards and isolated ephemeral sub-agent context. - Closure path: no first-party path was found that detects a specific interaction disturbance among two distinct S1 agents, selects an attenuation response for that disturbance, and feeds the coordination result back into subsequent local S1 behaviour. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: multi-agent reachability, routing and delegation are present, but the Methodology explicitly requires more than communication/handoff. The reviewed product runtime does not supply an S2-specific autonomous or constructor path tied to a concrete inter-S1 oscillation/conflict witness. - Evidence: README.md, agent/builtin.go, gateway/a2a/a2a.go. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no distinct first-party whole-system current-control function was established beyond local agent planning/delegation and deterministic runtime enforcement. - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate current-control paths inspected included store-backed plans, delegation, flow execution state, registry discovery, tool approvals, spend/step/loop limits and runtime stop/resume mechanisms. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the ordinary agent may revise its own task plan and delegate subtasks, but no separate product-runtime path was found with a whole-system current view plus authority to bargain/regulate shared resources, commitments or priorities on behalf of multiple operational units. - Decision owner: none established for S3 at the declared recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: plan memory, delegate routing, flow state/checkpoints, registry/client machinery, tool/step/loop/spend limits, approvals and cancellation/timeouts. - Closure path: no S3-specific whole-system feedback/intervention loop was established. Local plan updates return to the same agent&#39;s task execution; deterministic limits enforce preselected constraints. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a parent agent that decomposes one user request and delegates one subtask is not automatically S3. The reviewed evidence does not show a standard mode where an agent receives a whole-system operational view and owns ongoing resource/commitment intervention across independent S1s. - Evidence: agent/builtin.go, README.md, flow/. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: .github/loop has project-level planning/current-work behavior, but it belongs to the excluded repository-development organization and is not borrowed into the product-runtime S3 mapping.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: challenge a flow-step result through a distinct verification judgment and return rejection feedback into another operational attempt before the flow proceeds with the bounded result. - Disturbance / variety regulated: plausible but deficient step outputs that ordinary body execution would otherwise return without a separate grade-and-correction pass. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the Grader decides pass/fail and supplies corrective feedback; Verify threads rejection feedback into the next body attempt and records the final verification outcome. - Decision owner: Go Micro does not ship a sufficiently independent default autonomous auditor for the general case. LLMGrader reuses the flow model and only sees the latest result. The application must compose a grader with independence appropriate to the audited claim; Go Micro supplies the S3-specific verification/feedback closure. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: bounded Verify attempt loop, feedback-field injection, optional backoff, verification metadata, flow checkpoint/result state and optional LLMGrader helper. - Closure path: body produces candidate result → composed grader audits it → rejection/feedback enters the next body input → body retries → accepted or bounded-failure result becomes the flow state. - Claim being audited: whether the current flow-step output satisfies the deployment-defined rubric/ground truth before it is accepted as the step&#39;s result. - Ordinary reporting path: the body step returns its State as normal flow output. - Complementary access path: the first-party Grader seam invokes a separately composed grading actor after body completion; the deployment can give that actor external ground truth, raw artifacts or a distinct model/service, and Go Micro closes its verdict back into retry. - Independence boundary: independence is not guaranteed by the built-in LLMGrader; it is intentionally left to the developer-supplied Grader. This is why the state is C, not A. - Who acts on findings: flow.Verify deterministically reinjects grader feedback into the next body attempt; the operational body/agent then produces the corrected candidate. - Boundary reachability: flow.Verify, the Grader contract and feedback/retry closure are shipped public first-party flow primitives at the reviewed revision, not CI-only tests. A deployment only needs to supply the independent grading actor/ground-truth access. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the function-specific audit path and return loop are first-party, but the decisive sufficiently independent audit actor is not supplied in a standard autonomous mode. General custom-code extensibility is not the basis; the credit is for the dedicated Verify/Grader primitive whose semantics are verification judgment plus corrective return. - Evidence: flow/verify.go, flow/verifytest.go. - Basis: explicit - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: same-model LLMGrader alone is not treated as sufficient complementary independence. A deployment that wires only that helper may not operationally realize S3 even though the first-party constructor exists.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: detect that current operational capability cannot satisfy an external requirement, develop a service-level adaptation, materialize it, and return the new capability into subsequent agent operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new user needs not covered by existing registered services/tools; requests to extend or modify an existing system; design/code/compile failures encountered while materializing the capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Base mode — the micro chat agent decides that no existing service can handle the request and chooses microgenerateservice; the generation models choose the service architecture/handler changes. Parent mode — the user reviews the LLM-designed system proposal and decides at Generate? [Y/n] whether the adaptation should be materialized. - Decision owner: Base mode — the autonomous chat/generation model path. Parent mode — the human operator owns the decisive accept/reject adaptation judgment, while the generator autonomously develops and implements the approved option. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: registry/tool discovery, generate.Design, existing-service scan, deterministic service-structure generation, LLM handler generation, bounded compile-fix loop, process startup, registry wait and tool refresh. - Closure path: external capability request + current service inventory → missing-capability distinction → model designs service adaptation → autonomous choice or parent approval triggers generation → code is written/compile-fixed/built/started → registry/tool set refreshes → subsequent agent turns can use the new service. - External distinction: the user asks for a capability absent from the current registered service/tool environment, or asks to extend/modify the existing system. - Future / prospective distinction: Go Micro models a desired future system/service capability relative to the current inventory rather than only selecting among existing tools; Design explicitly receives existing services and constructs the complete extended target design. - Adaptation option generated: one or more new/modified Go Micro services with schema/endpoints, generated handler/business logic and an updated managing agent/tool surface. - Path back into current capability / S3: generated services are compiled and started, allowed to register, micro chat refreshes discovered tools, and the new endpoints become immediately usable by the same operational agent; in initial generation mode the approved system becomes the subsequent running service/agent environment. - Boundary reachability: both micro chat autonomous missing-capability generation and micro run --prompt / CLI user-approved generation are shipped documented CLI modes. The positive mapping does not depend on repository CI or .github/loop dogfood. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: in base mode removing the model&#39;s missing-capability judgment/service-design choices leaves only a generator with no contextual adaptation decision. In parent mode the human decides whether the proposed adaptation is admitted, and that decision is returned through the same generator into executable capability. - Evidence: cmd/micro/chat/chat.go, cmd/micro/cli/generate/generate.go, cmd/micro/run/run.go, README.md, flow/analyze.go. - Basis: explicit - Confidence: high - Caveats: the adaptation path is developer/runtime oriented rather than production hot-patching of an arbitrary deployed service; the credited mode is nevertheless first-party and explicitly returns generated service capability into the supported running micro chat environment. flow.Analyze/LLMOptimizer alone would be only a partial adaptation constructor because applying its prompt proposal is caller-owned.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no product-runtime identity/ultimate-policy decision loop was established at the declared recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate paths inspected included agent system prompts, auth/rules, tool approvals, spend/step/loop limits, requestinput, generated-service confirmation and repository North Star/maintainer restrictions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none of the product-runtime mechanisms escalates a genuine identity/ultimate-policy issue to a legitimate ultimate authority and returns that decision to govern subsequent operation at this recursion. - Decision owner: none established for S5 in the assessed product runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: auth rules/JWT wrappers, guardrails, tool approvals, static prompts/configuration, human input pauses, spend budgets and generation confirmation. - Closure path: ordinary task/action approvals and configuration changes can alter execution, but no identity/ultimate-policy issue → legitimate authority → authoritative decision → returned policy loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: policy text, permission checks and human approvals bound ordinary operations; they do not by themselves own system identity or ultimate policy. Repository NORTHSTAR and off-limits maintainer decisions belong to the excluded repository-development organization. - Evidence: agent/builtin.go, auth/, internal/docs/CONTINUOUSIMPROVEMENT.md. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9a9faa2e1208ecb2712c492398de7b7e7f24976a/assessments/go-micro.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9a9faa2e1208ecb2712c492398de7b7e7f24976a\">9a9faa2 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-11T21:55:22+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/WittyRyuji/WittyCode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">WittyCode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-11\">2026-11-11</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/WittyRyuji/WittyCode/tree/67af7d8fb0851cd4623c7b94993deddcb766ec1f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 67af7d8fb0851cd4623c7b94993deddcb766ec1f\">67af7d8 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: choose and carry out model-directed local coding and information actions for the user&#39;s request. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing local repository contents, search output, command errors, user steering, interrupted runs, provider rate limits, context pressure and ambiguous next steps. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the model chooses tool name and arguments, interprets returned observations and decides to continue or finish within the first-party multi-step loop. - Decision owner: selected API/ChatGPT-backed model in the shipped WittyCode agent runtime; the user sets the permission envelope. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ToolContext, command/file tool registry, approval/Plan restrictions, tool-output clipping, streaming adapters, retry and cancellation handling, persisted session log, compaction and step cap. - Closure path: user prompt → controller-created AgentLoop → model call → permitted local action → tool result appended as functioncalloutput → subsequent model choice or final response; /goal can add bounded further turns. - Boundary reachability: the README&#39;s packaged desktop first-party ChatGPT Plan/API Provider modes instantiate AgentLoop from the production controller; they do not require the external Codex or Devin CLI agent loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: action selection and iteration depend on model judgment over tool observations; permission checks and persistence enforce or transport that judgment rather than selecting the action. - Evidence: runtime loop and action return, production controller wiring, permissions, goal loop. - Basis: structural and explicit. - Confidence: high for first-party control wiring; medium for provider-side run behavior. - Caveats: source-level assessment, not a paid provider or Windows desktop E2E run; /goal uses a model self-verdict and fixed deterministic limits, not independent verification.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct first-party cross-S1 conflict-attenuation function is demonstrated at the selected one-session recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool calls, queued steering messages and permission requests within a single conversation may contend, but they do not constitute conflict between independent S1 operating units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: deterministic per-turn tool sequencing and session state transitions; no cross-unit coordination discretion is shown. - Decision owner: the single active model for its task, with deterministic controller sequencing; no separate S2 agent is present. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: serial runcalls, queued steering and session lifecycle state. - Closure path: one session&#39;s model calls are executed and observed in order; no distinct S1 collision is detected and returned as an inter-unit coordination adjustment. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: alternate model engines are choices for the same session, not simultaneously operating S1 units; sequential tool calls are within-S1 execution discipline. - Evidence: loop call execution, controller session lifecycle, engine overview. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: an independent multi-agent setup composed outside the packaged app is not assessed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no separately evidenced whole-current system control over a portfolio of S1 units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an individual session can run too long, fail, be stopped or require approval; these are local execution and user constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the user changes permission/model/mode; deterministic runtime caps steps, retries transient failures and stops/records turns. - Decision owner: user/configuration and local runtime enforcement for a single session, not an in-boundary agent or parent making cross-unit current-control decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MAXSTEPS, /goal round and idle caps, ChatGPTController state, ToolContext permissions and session persistence. - Closure path: run limit, failure or stop → session notice/termination or retry; no global current view → changed portfolio commitment/resource decision → subsequent S1 regulation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model&#39;s ordinary task choices and end markers are S1 work; the limits enforce preselected bounds rather than whole-system S3 discretion. - Evidence: controller, goal bounds, permissions. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a user&#39;s oversight of many separate local conversations is outside the credited single-session governing system.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no independent complementary audit of operational claims with a distinct corrective authority/return is evidenced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: wrong tool actions, memory-proposal errors, partial provider failures and user-visible claims may be examined by ordinary tools, user review or offline tests. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user approves individual tool actions or reviews stored memory proposals; tests and source reviews are maintainer/development activities. - Decision owner: user for ordinary approvals, same operating model for its own task, and external developers for offline quality review—not an independent runtime auditor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permission checks, session log and compaction invariants, memory review UI, local test suites, docs/harness-review- reports. - Closure path: approval/denial constrains a tool call, or offline tests inform developers; no complementary raw-evidence audit finding returns to a whole-system current controller. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a review label, permission check or maintainer&#39;s code inspection is not first-party independent audit of operational reality. - Evidence: review UI and memory proposals, permissions, development-only loop review. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: optional external human audit or testing may provide independent real-world assurance but is not included as an operational S3 path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no demonstrated external-and-prospective capability-adaptation cycle feeding current operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: model availability, provider errors, context pressure and imported user memory affect current tasks or operational settings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user chooses an existing engine/model; ordinary assistant may plan the current request; deterministic compaction adapts context size. - Decision owner: user/configuration for provider choices, model for immediate task execution; no separate prospective adaptation decision right. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider adapters, memory/import tools, skills discovery, context estimation and compaction. - Closure path: engine selection, memory refresh or compacted view can change the next turn; no evidence of sensing a future external distinction, developing strategic alternatives and selecting a changed organizational capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current-task research, retrying and session memory do not constitute outside-and-then intelligence under the Profile. - Evidence: README engines and local memory, loop compaction, goal current-task continuation. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: source does not preclude user-led prospective research in arbitrary prompts; no durable first-party S4 feedback path is demonstrated.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no evidenced runtime identity/ultimate-policy adjudication and authoritative returned governance rule. - Disturbance / variety regulated: permissions, project trust and write risk are bounded by fixed product constraints and user approvals for ordinary actions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: developer presets permission levels; user may approve/deny tools, select project or change model; no first-party actor is tasked with adjudicating the assistant&#39;s ultimate purpose/identity. - Decision owner: developer and user for configuration and ordinary consent, not a demonstrated S5 decision owner for an identity-level matter. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ask/code/full, Plan mode, protected paths, approval cards and system instructions. - Closure path: permission decision allows/denies a specific action; no identity-level proposal → legitimate ultimate authority → policy return governing future operation is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: static rules and task approvals constrain S1 but do not establish a substantive S5 identity decision loop. - Evidence: permissions and approval, session mode/instructions wiring, README. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: an external organization may impose identity governance on its deployment, but it is not shipped as a closed first-party mode here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/aeaee27fd7793297205479b5d86a56826341603b/assessments/wi/wittycode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit aeaee27fd7793297205479b5d86a56826341603b\">aeaee27 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-11T19:50:29+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/shalyhinpavel/go-agent-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Go Agent Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-11\">2026-11-11</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/shalyhinpavel/go-agent-harness/tree/362eef286581c67c8d384369d03873aad0807843\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 362eef286581c67c8d384369d03873aad0807843\">362eef2 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: convert user tasks into model-selected tool actions and completed conversational results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user requests, tool errors/content, changed file/web observations, stream interruptions and context-window pressure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose a registered tool and arguments after reading previous results, or end with an answer; subsequent iterations may revise the choice. - Decision owner: the configured external model acting through a first-party executable AgentEngine loop; Go infrastructure bounds execution but does not select semantic task steps. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool registry, provider adapters, parallel call executor, retry/compaction policy, session store, cancellation and opt-in approval. - Closure path: prompt/session → model tool call → executeToolsParallel → tool-result message persisted → next model decision or terminal answer / done chunk. - Boundary reachability: internal/bootstrap.BuildEngine wires the shipped CLI/server to NewAgentEngine, and StreamChat directly calls the configured provider and executes registered model-selected tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model has task-level discretion and receives real tool feedback; provider autonomy is credited only within the packaged invocation, not as Go deterministic ownership of model judgment. - Evidence: engine turn and model loop, tool result/continuation, parallel tool executor, CLI bootstrap. - Basis: structural, first-party shipped code; confidence: high for the described model-driven single-agent loop. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: source-static examination; no live provider quality, autonomy performance or model-level identity is inferred.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no first-party coordination relation regulating interference among distinct autonomous S1 work units is established in the standard runtime. - Disturbance / variety regulated: simultaneous calls and overlapping turns could conflict, but the shipped guards regulate one session&#39;s request lifecycle rather than negotiated interference between distinct operational agents. - Decisive decision or feedback right: BeginTurn rejects concurrent turns of one session; executeToolsParallel serializes result order and caps tool fan-out without an agent-level arbitration choice. - Decision owner: deterministic runtime for per-session exclusion/parallel tool mechanics; no distinct S2 owner or specifically composable inter-agent conflict controller shown. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session busy map, semaphore and stable result slots. - Closure path: conflicting same-session second request returns busy/error, while independent sessions execute separately; neither is a demonstrated S1-to-S1 coordination feedback loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: neither model nor configurable first-party mediation is shown to select an interference-specific coordination response across independent agent work units. - Evidence: session turn guard, parallel tool result ordering, bootstrap one-engine assembly. - Basis: bounded structural absence; confidence: medium-high. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: external applications can embed several engines and implement S2, but their coordination is not supplied by this distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no whole-system current-control decision loop over a portfolio of operational commitments and common resources is shipped. - Disturbance / variety regulated: session concurrency, context exhaustion, tool side effects and model iteration budget are controlled locally, but not through a whole-current organizational intervention. - Decisive decision or feedback right: configured limits and a caller-supplied Proceed hook constrain individual turns; no first-party actor surveys current multi-S1 commitments and chooses reassignment, resource bargain or exception intervention. - Decision owner: embedding developer/operator selects configuration, runtime enforces; optional human approves individual tools (ordinary execution risk, not whole-current S3). - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MaxIterations, MaxParallelTools, context guard, session TTL, tool approval and SSE broker. - Closure path: limit/approval refusal changes or ends an individual tool/turn, not a first-party system-wide current-control decision returned to multiple S1 operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: neither hard guard nor generic hook carries discretionary S3 current-control ownership on its own. - Evidence: config and hooks, limits and guards, bootstrap. - Basis: structural boundary-specific negative; confidence: medium. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: a host program may build current-control policy over many engines, but that is outside this default harness&#39;s accountable operating boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no shipped complementary independent audit judgment/return path beyond normal agent/tool reporting. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false tool-success claims or hidden operational errors are possible, but event trace alone does not audit the claim. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none shown: optional trace files record steps and errors; provider conformance check tests model adapters outside a production session. - Decision owner: normal engine records traces and configured user can inspect them later; no separate first-party auditor controls independent evidence and corrective return. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: JSONL trace.Sink, stream event chunks, unit tests and live provider conformance checker. - Closure path: logs are written for inspection/replay; no included audit decision demonstrably changes subsequent session operating authority. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model&#39;s own turn report and trace sink do not provide independent complementary judgment, and the separate checker belongs to adjacent testing. - Evidence: trace semantics and Record, provider conformance checker, bootstrap opt-in trace. - Basis: bounded negative after tracing candidate audit surfaces; confidence: medium-high. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: trace is useful primary material for an externally composed auditor, not evidence that one ships in this runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party outside-and-then prospective environmental option selection and capability-return loop identified. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing models, token limits and user memory affect current requests, not strategic future viability choices. - Decisive decision or feedback right: users/developers select providers and tools; auto-compaction summarizes past history, and memory ports retrieve user data rather than develop future capability options. - Decision owner: developer/operator configures providers, prompt and memory; the model owns current task steps, not a shipped forward-looking adaptation decision process. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PromptAugmenter, ContextCompactor, AutoCompact, memory store, MCP registration and model configuration. - Closure path: summarized history and user dossier enter a later model request; no evidenced external trend → evaluated prospective option → changed durable harness capability → S3 return path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: operational context preservation is not strategic learning/adaptation; a possible future custom hook is not a current first-party S4 constructor. - Evidence: compaction and prompt ports, memory bootstrap, README memory design. - Basis: bounded negative after prospective-path search; confidence: medium. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: model can reason about tomorrow if a user asks it to; this is task behavior, not an included organizational adaptation loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no packaged identity/ultimate-policy judgment and legitimate returned decision path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: action safety, file access, tool-level approvals and user/session isolation are runtime risk policy, not ultimate organizational mission disputes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: human may approve/deny a tool call and developer can change system prompt, but neither is a shipped process for escalating and resolving mission/identity-level questions. - Decision owner: external human/developer owns application purpose; Go runtime holds enforcement boundaries, not ultimate policy authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Approver, ApprovalPolicy, ReadOnly, bootstrap file/bash opt-ins, per-user session identity. - Closure path: tool approval returns a particular execution answer; no first-party route takes an identity/ultimate-policy matter to legitimate parent authority and returns a governing identity decision for future operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic restrictions and ordinary one-off human consent are not S5, nor is a static prompt. - Evidence: tool approval API, approval execution, server approval broker. - Basis: bounded negative; confidence: medium-high. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: application authors may add higher-recursion governance, not credited without a shipped first-party mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4704aac872cafd0512e5adf0e6ddc496bbbbf918/assessments/go/go-agent-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4704aac872cafd0512e5adf0e6ddc496bbbbf918\">4704aac &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-11T18:07:26+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/milletcode/milletcode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MilletCode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-11\">2026-11-11</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/milletcode/milletcode/tree/2278a648381e3279bb1f29ed54cf48ad19c44d66\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 2278a648381e3279bb1f29ed54cf48ad19c44d66\">2278a64 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: carry out model-directed coding/dialog tasks in configured project workspaces. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing prompts, code/tool observations, permissions, session context and test results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the Claude Code model chooses next edits, tools and responses during a live session; users may approve/deny restricted actions. - Decision owner: first-party-launched Claude Code agent process in an enabled instance, whose model reasoning is external but whose session/approval/feedback organization is shipped by MilletCode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ClaudeAdapter process launcher, SessionManager, JSONL event parser, approval response handling, SSH/local runtime and WebSocket streaming. - Closure path: browser/API prompt → launched Claude CLI with project context → model/tool work → streamed event or approval → user/CLI response → continued model step or completed session. - Boundary reachability: Claude adapter is enabled by default and SessionManager::createsession resolves its configured local or SSH executable; no custom third-party framework implementation is needed beyond installing Claude Code. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model selects content/steps and handles tool feedback; backend only starts, meters, routes and persists the session. - Evidence: Claude adapter launch/events, session management, session API. - Basis: structural shipped CLI agent mode. - Confidence: high for adapter-agent reachability, not for quality of model execution. - Caveats: no model-provider credentials or live end-to-end workflow tested; HTTP agent adapters are immature/disabled and not needed for the S1 claim.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no evidenced first-party discretionary regulation of interfering decisions among distinct running coding agents. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two agents could touch the same source files or make incompatible changes, but no inter-agent collision resolution loop is shipped. - Decisive decision or feedback right: per-session duplicate-key overwrite/resume and resource handling decide session identity, not cross-agent work-claim arbitration. - Decision owner: absent for an S2-specific corrective choice; operators manually coordinate tasks outside this provided relation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: composite session keys, status registry, SSH pooling, approval queue, task-specific stop/resume and notifications. - Closure path: no first-party scope conflict event → coordination verdict → changed instructions to two distinct S1 agents demonstrated. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: session identity deduplication and transport routing prevent duplicate sessions but do not attenuate conflicting independent tasks across agents. - Evidence: session manager, session API, multi-agent feature description. - Basis: bounded no-path within included session/agent/transport and project surfaces. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: an operator or external Claude agent could independently coordinate work; that is not the first-party default S2 loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: oversee and intervene in currently active sessions and project commitments across the operator&#39;s MilletCode installation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stuck/idle agents, unexpected approvals, errored sessions and competing current project work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: human operator can select projects/sessions, issue work messages, stop/resume instances, resolve pending approvals and change active execution configuration. - Decision owner: authenticated local/hosted human console operator; no model is shown prioritizing all sessions from a whole-deployment view. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dashboard and sessions routes, persistent session store, SessionManager, status APIs, pending-control registry, idle alerts and project discovery. - Whole-system current view: /api/sessions and projects console enumerate running/idle/stopped sessions across projects and agents, with current status and approval prompts. - Current-control decision scope: operator allocates attention and instructions, intervenes in stuck sessions and changes which active coding threads continue. - Closure path: aggregated session/project state → operator selects intervention/priority → REST action (message/approve/stop/restore) → changed agent process and subsequent dashboard state. - Boundary reachability: installed browser dashboard and REST session controls are first-party supported modes, not maintainer-only developer infrastructure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: whole-session decisions require human selection; automatic status updates and Lark notifications only report/enforce. - Evidence: sessions API, session manager, console. - Basis: direct parent-operated current-control path. - Confidence: medium-high for parent supervision; actual scheduling effectiveness untested. - Caveats: current S3 belongs to the deployed operator, not an autonomous model supervisor; a session&#39;s approval prompt alone would not prove S3 without the whole view.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no independent first-party review of coding-agent operational claims with separate evidence access and a corrective verdict demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: coding errors/false completion reports remain possible; installation validation and permission checks address different narrower hazards. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no shipped independent code-audit actor/constructor returns a judgment that changes worker behavior. - Decision owner: human may inspect artifacts externally, but that reviewer is not a first-party complementary path in this installed system. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session history, Git/file browser, per-action approvals and skill after-install checks. - Closure path: user can view output and decide manually; an independent audit → findings → corrective agent control loop is not supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: approval prompts are permission decisions, and existence checks for installed skills do not review agent code/work independently. - Evidence: sessions UI/API, skills verification, README. - Basis: bounded absence of independent S3 audit boundary. - Confidence: medium-high for standard distribution. - Caveats: external CI or a separately hired model reviewer could be attached, but is not credited without a first-party integration.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: select external skill packages as future coding-agent capability options and deploy the chosen adaptation across configured agent nodes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new external skill capabilities and changing local agent requirements beyond a current user prompt. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a human operator evaluates the available official/third-party skill option and requests install/uninstall; no autonomous model has blanket authority to define future capabilities. - Decision owner: human console operator who chooses a skill and, for official packages, provides/configures the trusted download source; model-run third-party installers execute that request within bounds. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: official skill manifest, skills marketplace UI, API install queues, configured archive source/password, per-node plan, verification after install, persistent status. - External distinction: skill packages originate outside the currently installed agent session and may introduce capabilities it previously lacked, unlike session state, logs or immediate code output. - Future / prospective distinction: the operator selects a reusable capability for subsequent sessions rather than merely answering an ongoing coding turn. - Adaptation option generated: displayed available skill packages plus manually supplied third-party skill requests; choice remains human and the catalog is not independent strategic research. - Path back into current capability / S3: operator skill selection → official/third-party install API → per-agent/node skill directory update and verification → sessions using newly available installed skills. - Closure path: offered outside skill option → parent selection/approval → installation/verification → changed agent skill availability → later coding operations. - Boundary reachability: first-party skills.rs routes and Skills UI are shipped; official install requires an explicitly configured source, while third-party install requires an operator-provided request. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: software/agents may execute installation, but the prospective option and authorization are owned by the operator, so parent P rather than A. - Evidence: skills API and install verification, skill market UI, README feature description. - Basis: first-party parent-governed capability acquisition path, narrow S4. - Confidence: medium; live source availability and installed-skill usefulness not tested. - Caveats: not general environmental foresight, adaptive product strategy or autonomous skill selection; no claim official downloads work without configured URL/password.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no evidenced first-party path where the installed deployment&#39;s ultimate mission/identity is authoritatively selected and returned into operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: credential/auth and agent allowlists constrain execution, but do not settle the ultimate purpose of the deployment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operator can configure access, default model, approvals and skills, but no specific ultimate-identity decision circuit is shown. - Decision owner: legitimate human owner retains general authority outside the runtime; operational permissions should not be mislabeled a closed S5 function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: config.toml, auth.mode, agents/instances, approval controls, SSH credentials and skill installation policy. - Closure path: settings change execution permissions and capacity, not an explicit identity-level mission/organizational purpose selection and return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no model owns policy identity, and human password/approval controls alone do not establish parent S5 by the function-first threshold. - Evidence: configuration, README configuration, approval API. - Basis: bounded no-path at installed boundary. - Confidence: medium; an operator&#39;s outside organizational identity is not denied. - Caveats: selecting a new skill as future capability is S4 P here, not itself S5 identity authority.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/70a43d08d80314385bda7c8aac6af10e0cdf8810/assessments/mi/milletcode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 70a43d08d80314385bda7c8aac6af10e0cdf8810\">70a43d0 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-11T17:36:00+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/devsohm/axi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Axi</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-11\">2026-11-11</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/devsohm/axi/tree/b1d0fe007535f1c59d08c1f06e014c4558a4862d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b1d0fe007535f1c59d08c1f06e014c4558a4862d\">b1d0fe0 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Complete a submitted task through model-selected actions and committed feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool outcomes, model errors, context limits, interruptions and partially complete work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select the next supported tool and its arguments, revise the action after tool observations, or return an answer. - Decision owner: The model bound to the packaged GenerationTask owns semantic choices. The harness owns phase execution and persistence. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Model collection, first-party provider adapters, ToolTask, registered tool factories, scheduler, views and checkpointed storage. - Closure path: Conversation submit → GenerationTask selects request → model calls a tool → ToolTask records result → next generation consumes result or commits completion. - Boundary reachability: Harness.open, root submission, GenerationTask and ToolTask are public supported distribution paths. The shipped provider can be configured in place of the tutorial faux provider. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The host selects provider and tool policy. The model makes per-task decisions through the supplied standard executor without a host-authored control loop. - Evidence: packages/durable/docs/tutorial.md, packages/durable/src/harness/harness.ts, packages/durable/src/harness/generation.ts, packages/durable/src/harness/generation.ts, packages/durable/src/tools/index.ts, packages/ai/src/api/openai-responses.ts - Basis: Pinned first-party source and declared distribution. - Confidence: High for the bounded structural finding. - Caveats: Source-static assessment. Provider behavior and real-world quality were not tested.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No autonomous coordination between distinct S1 units is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel conversations and file mutations can share resources. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Resolve inter-conversation interference and change subsequent operating choices across the units. - Decision owner: Scheduler and mutation queue serialize work under fixed rules. The host defines any inter-conversation policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Ownership graph, task scheduler, document transactions and canonical-file mutation queue. - Closure path: Locks and task ordering regulate writes and resource access. Results return to the originating tool or conversation. - Boundary reachability: Child conversations, task ownership and file tools are present. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The implemented lifecycle and write serialization do not expose a distinct cross-S1 decision/feedback role. - Evidence: packages/durable/src/harness/scheduler.ts, packages/durable/src/harness/ownership.ts, packages/durable/src/tools/file-mutation-queue.ts - Basis: Pinned first-party source and declared distribution. - Confidence: High for the bounded structural finding. - Caveats: Source-static assessment. Provider behavior and real-world quality were not tested.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No whole-system current-control decision mode is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Live tasks, stalled phases, conversation usage and cancellation requests. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Reallocate or change whole-system operating commitments with returned discretionary intervention. - Decision owner: The app chooses configuration. The scheduler manages present phases under fixed lifecycle rules. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task graph, views, checkpoints, metadata telemetry and cancellation. - Closure path: Current statuses drive scheduling, restart and cancellation within the lifecycle contract. - Boundary reachability: The public Harness exposes task inspection and conversation control primitives. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Observations and isolated operator controls leave the whole-system judgment and return to adopter code. - Evidence: packages/durable/src/harness/harness.ts, packages/durable/src/harness/scheduler.ts, packages/durable/src/harness/task-graph.ts, packages/durable/src/harness/view.ts - Basis: Pinned first-party source and declared distribution. - Confidence: High for the bounded structural finding. - Caveats: Source-static assessment. Provider behavior and real-world quality were not tested.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No complementary operational audit is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: An agent response or tool outcome may disagree with task reality. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Inspect alternative evidence and return a claim-specific corrective judgment. - Decision owner: Producing conversation receives committed tool results. Hosts own any telemetry or extra hook logic. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Recorded tool phases, transcript, durable telemetry and development test fixtures. - Closure path: Runtime records return to the same conversation or host subscribers. There is no audit verdict in standard generation settlement. - Boundary reachability: Packaged telemetry and task views expose event observations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Neither a hook nor a second arbitrary conversation carries audit-specific independence and return by default. - Evidence: packages/durable/src/harness/generation.ts, packages/durable/src/harness/telemetry.ts, packages/durable/src/harness/registry.ts, packages/durable/README.md - Basis: Pinned first-party source and declared distribution. - Confidence: High for the bounded structural finding. - Caveats: Source-static assessment. Provider behavior and real-world quality were not tested.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No prospective adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changes in model catalogs, context length or available extensions may affect future tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Sense outside change, judge a future capability option and feed it into present operations. - Decision owner: Host selects model configuration and extensions. Compaction maintains the active conversation context. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Model catalogs, compaction tasks, configuration and persisted context boundaries. - Closure path: The compaction output changes the next prompt. It does not commit a prospective capability adaptation. - Boundary reachability: Compaction is a supported conversation function. Catalog tooling belongs to model package maintenance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Selecting or summarizing context for current work does not assign outside-and-then ownership. - Evidence: packages/durable/src/harness/compaction.ts, packages/durable/src/harness/agent.ts, packages/ai/src/model-catalog.ts, packages/durable/docs/spec.md - Basis: Pinned first-party source and declared distribution. - Confidence: High for the bounded structural finding. - Caveats: Source-static assessment. Provider behavior and real-world quality were not tested.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No ultimate identity/policy decision process is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Host policy, request identity overrides and conversation-level settings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Resolve an identity-level dispute and apply the authoritative result to future operation. - Decision owner: The embedding application supplies policy and identity fields. Harness applies runtime configuration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Request identity, extension registry, AgentDoc settings and ownership checks. - Closure path: Configured identity and tool policy influence requests and run admission under predefined rules. - Boundary reachability: Harness.open and conversation configure are supported configuration paths. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An attribution string, permission boundary or configuration override does not substantiate ultimate authority arbitration. - Evidence: packages/durable/README.md, packages/durable/src/harness/harness.ts, packages/durable/src/harness/agent.ts, packages/durable/src/harness/ownership.ts - Basis: Pinned first-party source and declared distribution. - Confidence: High for the bounded structural finding. - Caveats: Source-static assessment. Provider behavior and real-world quality were not tested.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/09960790f90ed113da880c0571998635cbf1927e/assessments/ax/axi.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 09960790f90ed113da880c0571998635cbf1927e\">0996079 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-11T17:22:17+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Andy1890/ruhui-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">RuHui Agent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-11\">2026-11-11</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Andy1890/ruhui-agent/tree/4bbff9f3094f5024edb7e25ba36b4c31d55df7d6\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 4bbff9f3094f5024edb7e25ba36b4c31d55df7d6\">4bbff9f &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Carry out a local user task through iterative model-selected tool actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool failures, malformed output, incomplete workspace information and consumption limits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose the next supported tool and its arguments, revise after observations, or finish with text. - Decision owner: The model actor bound to the first-party CLI/SDK turn owns the semantic choices. The runtime owns dispatch and fixed limits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Provider adapter, context assembly, tool mode policy, local handlers, rollout and budget checks. - Closure path: Task → provider response → tool call → local observation or corrective hint → next provider step → completion or bounded stop. - Boundary reachability: CLI delegates to the SDK and the SDK binds the configured provider and executor before calling the core loop. The OpenAI-compatible adapter is an implemented mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The semantic action is selected from task and results by the model. The scripted provider is a fixture mode and does not substantiate agent ownership by itself. - Evidence: packages/cli/ruhui.mjs, packages/sdk/agent-node.mjs, packages/core/src/loop.ts, packages/providers/openai-node.mjs - Basis: Pinned first-party source and declared distribution. - Confidence: High for the bounded structural finding. - Caveats: Source-static assessment. Provider behavior and real-world quality were not tested.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No distinct S1 coordination is established in the local turn boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool order and per-workspace state can affect the current task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: An inter-operation conflict judgment would require independently operating S1 units sharing a relevant disturbance. - Decision owner: The active turn owns its tool decisions. The runtime sequences calls and keeps session state. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Serial tool dispatch, tool-batch concurrency flags and local rollout/session boundaries. - Closure path: Tool results return to the same turn. No lateral conflict signal is returned between S1s. - Boundary reachability: The CLI and SDK bind the same single-turn execution path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Ordering tools issued by one model is internal to S1. - Evidence: packages/core/src/loop.ts, packages/tools/src/runtime.ts, packages/sdk/agent-node.mjs - Basis: Pinned first-party source and declared distribution. - Confidence: High for the bounded structural finding. - Caveats: Source-static assessment. Provider behavior and real-world quality were not tested.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No separate current-control process over whole-system commitments is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Current turn cost, time and repeated tool failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A portfolio-level intervention would require a current view and discretion over commitments beyond the turn. - Decision owner: The local constructor defines budgets and stopping rules. The user initiates or resumes work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Consumption counters, failure caps, exit states and the CLI doctor. - Closure path: Counters and observations produce fixed continuation or stopping behavior for the current turn. - Boundary reachability: SDK run/resume and doctor are reachable. Their current scope is local session execution and readiness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Local budget guards execute configured rules inside S1. - Evidence: packages/core/src/loop.ts, packages/core/src/budget.ts, packages/sdk/agent-node.mjs - Basis: Pinned first-party source and declared distribution. - Confidence: High for the bounded structural finding. - Caveats: Source-static assessment. Provider behavior and real-world quality were not tested.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No complementary operational audit is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Potential disagreement between a task claim and actual workspace or tool outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Independently sample evidence for the claim and return a corrective judgment. - Decision owner: The producing turn receives its own tool results. Development checks are maintained separately from task operation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Rollout events, deterministic rollout checks and eval fixtures. - Closure path: Runtime feedback goes back to the original model. Offline checks have no first-party return into current task control. - Boundary reachability: The product exposes its own event stream. The evaluation scripts are repository development commands. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Observation and deterministic checking do not establish a separate claim-specific challenge and return in the shipped operating mode. - Evidence: packages/core/src/loop.ts, packages/sdk/agent-node.mjs, scripts/eval.mjs, scripts/data/rollout.mjs - Basis: Pinned first-party source and declared distribution. - Confidence: High for the bounded structural finding. - Caveats: Source-static assessment. Provider behavior and real-world quality were not tested.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No prospective capability adaptation is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Workspace context and immediate changes to task inputs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Observe external change, generate a future option and return it into current capability. - Decision owner: The user chooses workspace instructions and configuration. The active model addresses the current task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Context discovery, configuration loading and session continuation. - Closure path: The context is assembled for the next local turn without an outside-and-then adaptation path. - Boundary reachability: Workspace and home configuration are loaded in supported modes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Task plan updates and context assembly do not govern future capability. - Evidence: packages/core/src/context.ts, packages/sdk/agent-node.mjs, packages/tools/src/plan.ts - Basis: Pinned first-party source and declared distribution. - Confidence: High for the bounded structural finding. - Caveats: Source-static assessment. Provider behavior and real-world quality were not tested.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No ultimate identity or policy arbitration process is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Local tool permission and instruction constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Resolve a conflict in governing identity or ultimate policy and change operating commitments. - Decision owner: The embedding caller or user configures the runtime, and deterministic tool policy applies the selected mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool policy, local configuration, user-input result and sandboxed workspace helpers. - Closure path: Configured rules gate the current tool invocation. No first-party identity arbitration precedes those constraints. - Boundary reachability: The first-party dispatcher applies mode policy. The user can respond to task-level requests. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Per-action permission and external configuration do not constitute a sourced ultimate-policy decision mode. - Evidence: packages/tools/src/policy.ts, packages/tools/src/runtime.ts, packages/sdk/agent-node.mjs, schema/config.schema.json - Basis: Pinned first-party source and declared distribution. - Confidence: High for the bounded structural finding. - Caveats: Source-static assessment. Provider behavior and real-world quality were not tested.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3de8817a771caf386c6598c21360f1d64c98eca8/assessments/ru/ruhui-agent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3de8817a771caf386c6598c21360f1d64c98eca8\">3de8817 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-08T15:35:01+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/vokramd/markov-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">markov-harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-08\">2026-11-08</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/vokramd/markov-harness/tree/6ffac34f74e6ee9c1e93cdff145c8d7656a0d769\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 6ffac34f74e6ee9c1e93cdff145c8d7656a0d769\">6ffac34 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: interactive model-selected repository reading, editing, shell execution and bounded specialist delegation in response to user coding requests and tool feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar workspace files, command failures, tool denials and evolving coding subtasks within a user session. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the configured LLM chooses substantive tool invocations and next action after reading real results; a child model separately selects its own allowed tools for a bounded delegated task. - Decision owner: main model/agent and, in the supported agent-tool mode, an explicitly invoked specialist model with its own session and tool permissions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: declarative repeat steps, first-party history assembly, read/edit/bash tools, hooks.py gate, optional human approval, rules, container boundary, context compaction and session/memory snapshot. - Closure path: model tool-use blocks enter gate and first-party tool workflows; outputs are recorded as tool-result content in the session; next agentstep call receives updated history and may correct/retry or finish. Child agent.run returns a tool result to main agent. - Boundary reachability: bin/harness invokes Markov with the shipped workflow; normal main -&gt; turn -&gt; agentstep and optional model-produced tools.agent -&gt; agent.run -&gt; subagentstep are implemented, not only illustrated in a test. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-dependent choice of tool, arguments and continuation is decisive S1 cognition; predetermined Markov repeat/gates constrain it but do not select individual coding actions. - Evidence: normal turn loop, model/tool feedback, delegated model loop, tool return. - Basis: first-party workflow/source evidence and unit tests; no live model-relay E2E in this assessment. - Confidence: high for the installed S1 model/tool return wiring; not an empirical model-success measurement. - Caveats: a separately installed external Markov engine, Podman and relay credentials are required. The bundled demo and provider claims are not promoted into independent verified operating evidence.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no credited inter-S1 disturbance-specific mutual adjustment with corrective feedback among independent specialists. - Disturbance / variety regulated: possible tool/workspace clashes between separately invoked specialist agents, but no first-party coordination of those clashes is established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no distinct mediation function shown; the main agent selects delegation and the workflow runs child calls one at a time. - Decision owner: main agent owns task assignment as part of its S1, and deterministic workflow settings own read-only parallelism/mutating serialization; no independent S2 decision owner is evidenced. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runreadonly concurrency 4, runmutating concurrency 1 and sequential runagents, per-tool permission checks and shared worktree. - Closure path: each child completes and reports back to the main model as a tool result; no unresolved conflict representation, peer settlement, reallocation or collision-specific adjustment drives another independent S1 unit. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: generic delegation, deterministic serial execution and permissions may avoid some collisions mechanically but do not establish VSM S2&#39;s interference/attenuation/feedback relation. - Evidence: serialized subagent calls, subagent result flow, README explicitly one-at-a-time. - Basis: source-level workflow modes and supported subagent documentation. - Confidence: high for lack of credited coordination within inspected shipped modes. - Caveats: this does not deny that serial execution is a valuable safety measure; it is not evidence of an independently agent-owned S2 function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no separate whole-harness current control over multiple independently committed S1 cells is established beyond the main agent&#39;s task-level delegation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool budget, blocked commands, long transcript, subagent termination and user approvals, all at session-operation level. - Decisive decision or feedback right: main model may call a child tool, while human may approve an individual edit/command; no separate whole-system controller can evaluate current multi-S1 commitments and reallocate them. - Decision owner: main task S1 and deterministic workflow limits, not a demonstrated whole-system S3 actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: maxtoolrounds, maxturns, approve rules, session continuation and summary compaction. - Closure path: child result folds back into main transcript, tool denial folds into the same agent, and compaction changes that agent&#39;s context; there is no cross-cell S3 resource/commitment closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: main-agent operational planning is not automatically promotion to autonomous S3; approval per operation is not parent whole-system control. - Evidence: single-session orchestration, subagent run-and-return, session settings. - Basis: first-party workflow and task/agent mode inspection. - Confidence: medium-high within reviewed distribution. - Caveats: an operator could externally run several instances or coordinate them; such an added organization is not contained in the pinned harness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no separate independent completed-work reality audit whose finding closes into corrective operational control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: hallucinated file updates or false task completion claims would require independent verification, but reviewed validation paths do not own such audit. - Decisive decision or feedback right: permission gate checks whether a proposed command/edit may run, and PostToolUse hooks may return suggestions; no default reviewer independently measures final claimed deliverables. - Decision owner: user approval and developer-authored hook rules for pre/post tool constraints; neither is an independent auditor of the task&#39;s outcome. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: hooks.py, edit diff and validation, tool-result feedback, local script tests, optional model memory extraction. - Closure path: gating sends allow/deny to normal tool execution; memory extraction summarizes conversation history to memory files, not verified real results with independent corrective verdicts. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: pre-execution policy checking and transcript-derived memory are not a distinct reality-reading audit of the completed S1 operation. - Evidence: tool gate and hooks, edit planning, memory extraction workflow. - Basis: code path and self-test review. - Confidence: high for absence of a first-party standalone reviewer within inspected modes. - Caveats: a user might install custom hooks performing validation; an external customized hook is not an established first-party independent audit owner.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no closed first-party external-environment intelligence and prospective organizational adaptation strategy, beyond retrieval/persistence of task memories. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future unfamiliar tasks/user preferences, which the memory files might help with but do not constitute an agent-selected organizational strategy loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model may extract memorable preferences/facts from past conversation, but no explicit future environmental option is selected, governed and enacted as a strategic adaptation. - Decision owner: ordinary main task model/optional extraction model for memory text; operator/developer authors permanent workflows, tools and settings. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: extract.memories, extract.step, memory.py index and snapshot, user/working-directory instructions, skills lookup and context compaction. - Closure path: extraction stores a fact file and future session prompt may incorporate its index; this is persistent S1 context, not a wider outside-and-then metasystem decision returned into changed organizational capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: retained instructions/memories can influence later S1 but have no evidenced externally oriented prospective scanning, alternative selection or strategy implementation closure. - Evidence: memory extraction trigger, extract fork and model pass, persistent memory context. - Basis: workflow and storage/permission paths. - Confidence: medium-high, with the memory mechanism reviewed as plausible false positive. - Caveats: capability to modify arbitrary project files does not by itself establish S4; no change to the first-party configured harness is automatically enacted by extracted facts.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no distinct legitimate ultimate policy/identity decision authority is closed inside the evaluated agent harness. - Disturbance / variety regulated: conflicting system purposes or durable constitution revisions would require a policy-identity adjudicator; source instead installs a fixed identity and user-written instruction precedence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no model or parent governance agent can authoritatively choose a constitutional identity for the harness; the user edits prompt/config/CLAUDE.md settings outside the agent. - Decision owner: installer/operator and original prompt author define static identity; normal agents select code actions within that envelope. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: vars/prompts.yaml static clientidentity/systemprompt, instructions.py snapshots and folder precedence, settings.py permission rules, user approvals. - Closure path: owner-edited instruction/settings text may be loaded in a later session, but no operating first-party identity-level adjudication and authoritative change cycle is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: routine permission rules and human approval of one command are operational constraints, not ultimate policy S5; static instructions are necessary identity input but not a decision owner on their own. - Evidence: static prompt variables, instruction snapshot, permission policy. - Basis: source-level prompt/authority and instruction path review. - Confidence: medium-high within first-party runner configuration. - Caveats: parent-maintainer GitHub governance or a user&#39;s own policy choices are outside the installed execution boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/702d486442ef1450bc6c56b4410c04642507a375/assessments/ma/markov-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 702d486442ef1450bc6c56b4410c04642507a375\">702d486 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"3\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-08T15:53:17+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/FuzzyKuji/OpenProgram\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenProgram</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>3</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-08\">2026-11-08</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/FuzzyKuji/OpenProgram/tree/447264865e871e30e7f638b42cccfe25d826f8d0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 447264865e871e30e7f638b42cccfe25d826f8d0\">4472648 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: model-directed coding, web/file/shell operations, multi-turn reasoning, and delegated specialist execution with concrete tool-result feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing user tasks, unknown files and webpages, failed tool actions, incomplete implementation results, and context-dependent follow-up instructions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an invoked model chooses actual model response/tool calls and how to continue after receiving results; a child/peer model chooses its own assigned work actions inside its own branch. - Decision owner: active configured Agent model for each conversation; user-selected provider is an external implementation dependency. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent Python call API, model loop/tool dispatch, tool registry, permission gate, conversation DAG, context assembly, Job/run state, file/edit/shell/web tools and optional worktree context. - Closure path: user or another authorized Agent supplies a task; runtime assembles current context, model requests tool calls, first-party tool dispatch obtains observations/errors, and the next model turn may repair, retry, delegate or conclude. Background child completion returns to the invoking conversation. - Boundary reachability: public openprogram CLI/web entrypoints reach first-party Agent runtime and registered tools; agent tool reaches runagentturn/runagentturnasync; child work is admitted as canonical Job rather than only documented in a scenario. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: tool selection and next-step adaptation are model decisions; deterministic permissions and execution adapters support them without becoming the S1 decision maker. - Evidence: Agent call, tool dispatch, child execution, registered tools. - Basis: shipped executable source, component/unit tests and documented standard invocation; no credentialed live-model E2E. - Confidence: high for first-party tool/model return wiring; provider success rates and operational safety are not measured. - Caveats: account setup, configured model and requested tool permissions are required. Python Agent instance methods are not automatically model tools unless explicitly registered; claims of universally automatic self-programming must not be generalized to every mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate capacity interference among separate active Agent conversations/Jobs and give a worktree-using coordinator an explicit integration/conflict feedback surface. - Disturbance / variety regulated: independent child/peer Jobs contending for global/session execution slots, simultaneous claims of the same queued work, and incompatible edits from multiple worktrees when integrating source changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: first-party capacity governor selects eligible queued Job claims and defers saturated sessions; an appropriately equipped coordinator model can then respond to a typed mergeconflict/notfastforward result by revising or deferring an integration. - Decision owner: deterministic Job governor for scheduling/capacity settlement; configured coordinating Agent for the optional subsequent worktree-repair decision, not an automatically present global LLM mediator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: durable Job admissions, claimnext oldest-eligible selection, per-session/global capacity, lease/worker lock, worktree lifecycle states, conflict-returning mergeworktree, and joboutput/worktree tools. - Closure path: conflicting Job demand is admitted/queued; saturated sessions are skipped until slots release, and an eligible peer eventually starts with fresh execution context. In the optional worktree mode, conflicting source branches remain active on typed failed merge; the caller receives the error and can ask its agent to change/retarget work before reattempt. - Boundary reachability: both concurrent Jobs and worktree operations are registered, exercised first-party execution paths; component tests cover skipping an older saturated session, dual claim prevention and failed merge preserving active worktree. This is a composable coordination relation, not evidence of an automatic model conflict-resolution team on all fresh installs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the affected units are individually model-backed S1 branches, while the attenuation is primarily an implemented deterministic subsystem. C credits this cross-S1 facility and optional model composition; it does not claim S2=A autonomously negotiates conflicting task intentions. - Distinct S1 units: independent model-backed subagent or saved Agent conversations whose Jobs can be live concurrently. - Inter-S1 disturbance: actual global/session execution saturation and competing edits from distinct worktrees, not abstract task ordering. - Attenuating coordination relation: capacity reservations/eligibility prevent exceeding limits, skip one saturated session to make another eligible, and worktree isolation plus typed merge failure prevents blind overwrite during integration. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: released capacity starts the next Job; failed worktree merge preserves the branch and exposes its error to the agent/tool caller so subsequent S1 work can repair before merging. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the governor&#39;s admission decisions explicitly depend on cross-Job capacity interference and the worktree merge path explicitly represents cross-edit collision; mere sendmessage, a shared DAG or parallel queries are not credited. - Evidence: cross-session claim loop and limits, conflict feedback/active branch retention, dispatch tests, worktree failure tests. - Basis: first-party dispatcher, worktree manager and executable component tests. - Confidence: medium-high for bounded mechanical conflict attenuation; low for an unconfigured agent-driven negotiation loop. - Caveats: path warnings outside worktree are not a hard sandbox, and Git itself performs the merge. Capacity scheduling does not semantically detect two tasks editing the same path. The optional model retry is a possible feedback response, not an observed autonomous repair success.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: in a configured coordinator mode, maintain an owner-workspace view of current work, agent status and Jobs, select next responsibility and reassign/delegate based on returned work status. - Disturbance / variety regulated: overdue/in-flight work, idle specialist conversations, blocked tasks, active/queued Jobs, failed handoffs and changing immediate user priority. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the Coordinator/Chief of Staff model may read open work and current agent/job state, decide assignment or next task and send a bounded handoff; recipient execution and reply flow back into later coordinator turns. - Decision owner: owner-selected coordinating Agent model; human enables always-on and chooses its permissions/mission; Job governor enforces but does not choose project priorities. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: saved Coordinator and Chief of Staff system/tool configurations, listagents, openwork, listjobs/joboutput, handoff/sendmessage, always-on wake/check-in and return notices, persisted Goal/Job status. - Closure path: a live status or scheduled wake enters the coordinator conversation; it reads available work and cross-agent status, selects next owner/step and sends an authorized handoff; target executes in its independent Agent context and sends a completion reply, which alters the next current-control view. - Boundary reachability: the shipped createbuiltinagents installs roles with planning/status/delegation tools; owner can enable a saved Agent&#39;s always-on mode; handoff and Job return are implemented with recovery and loop limits. Neither an installed template nor a default disabled ticker automatically proves every instance runs that coordinator. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current cross-work allocation is decided by a configured model rather than by a fixed queue; C reflects opt-in composition and limited scope rather than a universally active autonomous S3 manager. - Whole-system current view: for the owner-selected Agent portfolio, open work, addressable conversations, in-flight Job state and returned status; not an external organization&#39;s finances or all agents on other users&#39; instances. - Current-control decision scope: prioritization and delegation among current user-visible jobs, targeted recovery/escalation and reporting, bounded by saved Agent permissions and owner instructions. - Evidence: Coordinator and Chief of Staff built-ins, built-in installation, durable handoff and reply, always-on wake loop. - Basis: first-party configurable role registry and execution feedback; component tests of handoffs and wake turns. - Confidence: medium for the constructed S3 control loop; default-runtime universal coverage is explicitly not claimed. - Caveats: resource-capacity governor alone is not model-owned S3, and current delegated task summaries are not independent audit. All built-in always-on roles begin disabled; owner must configure and activate the relevant mode.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: optionally attach a separate read/test-only PR Verifier Agent to inspect a producer&#39;s actual work and return a corrective verdict to the coordinating/producing conversation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a producer&#39;s untested, incorrect or misleading completion claim may otherwise be accepted from its own status summary. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the separate PR Verifier model reads the specification, code/diff and real test outputs, then returns ship, fix first or reject and numbered findings; parent/producer may decide corrective work. - Decision owner: separately configured verifier conversation for the judgment; parent agent/operator for what action to take on findings, not a hard automated PR merge veto. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: distinct pr-producer/pr-verifier built-in AgentSpecs, verifier allowlist without write/edit tools, handoff/return-reply, named conversation/branch, worktree list and read/terminal access. - Closure path: producer records work and requests a separate verifier handoff; verifier reads actual shared project files and runs checks independently of the producer&#39;s narrative; its reply arrives back at the sender for rework or operator delivery. - Boundary reachability: the installed verifier has explicit read/test permissions and no write/edit tools, and real first-party handoff transport returns a separate Agent&#39;s result. This is composable when invoked; there is no default mandatory reviewer before a Git merge or update. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: independent evaluation belongs to a different model-backed conversation with separate tool rights. A model merely summarizing its own tests or the deterministic update installer verifier alone would not establish this claim. - Claim being audited: the producer&#39;s assertion that a change implements the handed specification and passes its tests. - Ordinary reporting path: producer&#39;s task completion, change summary and claimed test results, as received by its parent. - Complementary access path: verifier can inspect files/diff through read and code-navigation tools and invoke read-only test commands via shell, without relying only on producer&#39;s summary. - Independence boundary: separate AgentSpec, conversation/turn and read-only allowlist; not a different GitHub organization, independent account, trusted third-party audit, or a proven mutually untrusted sandbox. - Who acts on findings: configured coordinating agent, producer on a subsequent task, or local human owner receives the returned verdict and may request correction or block delivery. - Evidence: producer vs verifier roles and allowlists, returning handoffs, handoff end-to-end test, independent self-update controller boundary as separate non-agent safety evidence. - Basis: constructed separate reviewer path plus source-level code/tests; no observed live LLM reviewer run or enforced reviewer acceptance gate. - Confidence: medium for configurable complementary access; low for automatic per-change independent assurance. - Caveats: role prompt forbids editing but a permitted bash may alter files; independent read/test intent is not a sandboxed read-only process guarantee. The distinct self-update verifier is deterministic controller-side verification, not automatically an S3 model auditor for every user task.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no verified closed agent-owned chain that senses external future change, selects a strategic capability/market option and returns that choice as an enduring adaptation of the managed agent organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future tools, models, changing external knowledge and workflow requirements could require adaptation, but inspected functionality does not establish the full strategic closure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no single first-party, reachable, closed prospective option-selection right identified. The Research Desk may search current sources and report; arbitrary agent code edits may create new tools, but they are not automatically coupled into a future-intelligence decision loop. - Decision owner: owner/configured agents decide what to research or edit, with ultimate option integration normally requiring task instructions or user permission. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: optionally always-on Research Desk, websearch/webfetch, persistent Memory, first-party program hot-loader/self-programming methods, manually editable AgentSpecs. - Closure path: external updates can reach research briefs and user/agent actions; however the shipped role and tool wiring does not guarantee a future-oriented choice that changes strategy/capability and then verifies the enduring return to execution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: external browsing is sensing, reusable self-programmed methods are extensibility and current S1 programming; their existence in the same repository is not proof of a separate S4 cycle. - Evidence: Research Desk prompt/tool role, opt-in always-on scheduler, Agent method registration, ordinary agent class. - Basis: supported role/module source plus explicit absence of a coupled strategic decision/return path. - Confidence: medium within default and declared always-on modes; does not rule out a user-composed S4 program. - Caveats: the README&#39;s self-evolution and proactive-agent promises are not treated as proof of completed outside/then organizational learning.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: owner-controlled, durable change of the saved Agent&#39;s constitutive instructions, identity, permissions and standing goal, with the updated configuration read into subsequent execution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: conflict between an Agent&#39;s persistent purpose/permission boundary and the owner&#39;s desired identity or authorization regime, not a one-off shell approval. - Decisive decision or feedback right: local human owner may edit/revoke Agent configurations and decide whether a specialist can act, research or send externally; Agent cannot legitimately grant itself unrestricted policy rights. - Decision owner: parent local user/administrator of the OpenProgram workspace; the Model and settings UI can suggest/configure but are not a new autonomous S5 authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: versioned saved AgentSpec configuration, agentregistry.manager.update, selected tool allowlists, alwayson enabled/autonomy/goal fields and permission-mode enforcement, tool resolution into runtime request. - Closure path: authorized owner changes saved Agent policy/identity; registry stores the edited spec; later selected Agent calls resolve the updated tool/system instructions and execute under the new policy. A deleted built-in is not silently recreated on restart. - Boundary reachability: the normal installed UI/API exposes saving/updating AgentSpecs; the same registry is used to construct future Agent runtime and tool policy. This is a human P mode, not S5=A agent self-constitutional authority. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ultimate legitimate acceptance is a human choice; autonomous delegated task reassignment or self-programming cannot, by itself, rewrite the top-level owner&#39;s authority. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: persistent AgentSpec instructions/allowlist and alwayson.autonomy define what a named Agent is allowed and tasked to do across turns, distinct from a single conversation response. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: local human owner is the only credited ultimate actor; the configured agent remains subordinate even if able to propose changes. - Return-to-operation path: manager.update persists the edited AgentSpec, and Agent.calloptions/runtime tool resolution uses the spec when the Agent runs again. - Evidence: saved configuration schema, config update, built-in deletion and edit persistence, AgentSpec policy resolution into calls. - Basis: first-party persisted config source and operating endpoint architecture. - Confidence: medium-high for genuine human-controlled persistent Agent identity; no model-origin constitutional proposal/independent appeal workflow is claimed. - Caveats: updating a child Agent task prompt is usually ordinary S3 delegation, not S5. Credit applies narrowly to authorized durable changes of the governing Agent&#39;s own identity/rules, not to OpenProgram maintainers editing GitHub source.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c53852df302ccf9fe3ef533635d16b6611a6879a/assessments/op/openprogram.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c53852df302ccf9fe3ef533635d16b6611a6879a\">c53852d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"3\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-08T16:07:10+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/rainyZhou2/SreAgentHarness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">go-sre-harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>3</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-08\">2026-11-08</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/rainyZhou2/SreAgentHarness/tree/218c2ce880cbbda33df6464b78aef96d201be913\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 218c2ce880cbbda33df6464b78aef96d201be913\">218c2ce &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: model-directed incident investigation through plans, hypotheses and tool calls, with observed system state and errors feeding the next reasoning step. - Disturbance / variety regulated: novel user incidents, unknown root cause, heterogeneous SRE tools, contradictory evidence, tool failure and interrupted/retried attempts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: configured cognitive/LLM strategy selects hypothesis, tool call and next reasoning step; a model planner may choose a runnable flow and revise it after failure. - Decision owner: active model-backed strategy/planner inside the worker, constrained by tenant policy; scripted strategy is a deterministic smoke mode and is not counted as model autonomy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Run/Step/Attempt transactional ledger and leases, strategies, prompt manifest, model gateway, policy gate, ToolCall, flow compiler, retries, memory retrieval, evidence refs and tool observations. - Closure path: incident goal is persisted; worker obtains a fenced step lease and assembles context; model selects a real tool or conclusion; tool executes after safety/approval evaluation, writes output, and the next leased reasoning step can adjust hypotheses, plan or finish. - Boundary reachability: normal server/local entrypoints initialize the worker with cognitive strategies, tool registry, gateway and planner; a configured model and granted external tools reach the real execution path, while local scripted strategy can demonstrate plumbing only. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: action/hypothesis choice is model-cognitive rather than authored runbook; policy and transactional persistence constrain/record, but do not choose the agent&#39;s investigative next action. - Evidence: agent loop, loop safeguards and tool result, model/planner strategy, flow child execution. - Basis: pinned first-party Go source and declared entrypoints; no compiled/runtime or credentialed model E2E. - Confidence: medium-high for reachable source-level execution; operational correctness unverified. - Caveats: a rule-based run without an LLM does not prove an autonomous S1 choice; the source has no Go unit tests, and the available environment has no go compiler.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: prevent several independently operating incident agents from exceeding an operator-defined shared-tool/target action budget, and return typed denial feedback for subsequent reasoning. - Disturbance / variety regulated: separate run/child agents attempting repeated restarts/side effects against the same target within a tenant or exhausting concurrent worker capacity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an optional rate policy consults shared tenant action-intent history for a tool/target/window and applies configured deny or approval; pool/lease mechanics additionally prevent over-admission of active execution. - Decision owner: operator-authored RuleEngine and deterministic policy/work scheduler; an executing model can react to the refusal but has no right to relax the floor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-target/per-tool Rate match and PostgreSQL/local CountActions, fail-closed history errors, action-intent ledger, policy gate, resource pool, serialized branch writer, typed refusal observations. - Closure path: first incident Run commits a relevant action intent; a concurrent second Run asks for the same tool/target; the shared counter reports the activity, rule matches threshold and gates action; denial/approval requirement returns to the second agent&#39;s next step. Activity ages out of the rule window, so later executions can become eligible. - Boundary reachability: RuleEngine.EvaluateRequest is a runnable gate and store implementations count persisted tenant intents; tenant policy configuration may enable rate.per=&quot;target&quot;. This is not a claim that a default fresh install has a protective rate rule for every SRE tool. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: C credits an implemented but configured cross-S1 interference/feedback mechanism; it is not A because the system does not evidence autonomous model negotiation of contention policies. - Distinct S1 units: separately executing incident Runs and flow-child model agents within the same tenant, sharing SRE action targets. - Inter-S1 disturbance: risk of repeated real side effects on the same service/target within one time window, caused by multiple agents (the mechanism also works with one agent). - Attenuating coordination relation: shared target-scoped action count conditions the second action gate on the first agent&#39;s recorded intent, reducing repeated/colliding tool effects. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: denial/approval status and reason are returned as a refused action in the model&#39;s observation stream; operator approval or elapsed time changes future eligibility. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: rate.per=&quot;target&quot; explicitly inspects real overlapping tool/target history across all tenant runs rather than merely passing messages, serializing one child or applying unrelated safety checks. - Evidence: target-aware rate contract/counter, rate-rule evaluation, shared SQL count, refusal feedback. - Basis: first-party code-level cross-run rule and durable ledger; no collision experiment executed. - Confidence: medium: explicit source-specific path but behavior depends on configured policy and functioning persistent store. - Caveats: rate windows are not atomic reservations against two concurrent first intents; both may pass before either records intent. Tenant-level policy protects only matching configured tool/targets and does not prove universal S2 negotiation.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: retain an instance-wide current view of lease/run/approval queue liveness and automatically recover stalled operations while preserving the worker and policy contract. - Disturbance / variety regulated: lost messages, worker crashes/expired leases, stuck conversation branches, stale queues and expired approval requests across simultaneously active incident runs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: first-party Reconciler.Tick reads global persistent state and decides which expired leases/approvals/stalled branches to reactivate or expire; an instance pool caps active work independently of model suggestions. - Decision owner: deterministic owner-instance reconciler and configuration, not a demonstrated LLM portfolio executive; user/operator owns the parameters. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: database Run/Step state, expired lease recovery, approval expiration, stalled-branch activation, stale run rewake, pool semaphores and notifications/outbox. - Closure path: durable state and timeouts make blocked/stale runs visible to the periodic reconciler; it returns leases to recoverable state, expires approval, wakes a run/branch and reconnects the next worker step to the same run ledger. - Boundary reachability: server app initializes the scheduler/reconciler in the normal service mode; the implementation operates on all relevant store runs for an instance, not a test-only runbook. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: first-party live control is deterministic and bounded; C is a composable organizational current-control facility, not S3=A model choice over enterprise priorities. - Whole-system current view: store-wide run leases, approval expirations, queued and stalled branch state inside the configured tenant/server boundary; excludes external production estate health, personnel or business portfolio. - Current-control decision scope: recovery/expiration/re-dispatch and per-process capacity, not redefinition of incident strategy, global budget allocation or policies. - Evidence: reconciler sweep, concurrency backpressure, durable state recovery. - Basis: first-party service code and durable semantics; no service-level restart fault-injection performed. - Confidence: medium-high for bounded S3-like runtime control, low for model-managed all-incident resource decisions. - Caveats: distinction from S2: S3 here reconciles the whole current execution state, not conflicts between two specific operational cells. A service monitor does not establish human-equivalent governance autonomy.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: check an agent&#39;s final incident claims against actual cited observations using a kernel-owned rule layer, with an optional separate model judge and bounded corrective backtracking. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unsupported self-declared incident resolution, fabricated citations, references to failed tool calls, inconsistent verification and conclusion/evidence mismatch. - Decisive decision or feedback right: verifier decides SUPPORTED/INSUFFICIENT/CONTRADICTED from a canonical evidence map; configured distinct judge may perform semantic support checks without being given the original agent&#39;s prompt/history; kernel can return a challenge step to the producing model. - Decision owner: non-agent rule verifier by default and optional independent model judge; kernel for backtrack/action decision, not the producing strategy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: verify.Verifier, ModelJudge with a separate alias/prompt, canonical citation resolver, internal conclusionreview follow-up, bounded MaxBacktracks, durable independentverification report and reasoning score gating. - Closure path: final task decision exposes structured claims; kernel reconstructs cited evidence from real observation refs, verifier evaluates it; on insufficient/contradicted status and remaining budget, a kernel-owned challenge returns issues to S1 reasoning and revised claims can be checked again. Otherwise report persists with an unsupported outcome. - Boundary reachability: normal server internal/app defaults HARNESSREASONINGVERIFY=true, and local runtime installs the rule verifier; setting HARNESSVERIFIERMODELALIAS adds a separate model judge. Hence C covers both genuine compulsory mechanical check and explicitly configured complementary model path, not a claim of always-on independent semantic LLM audit. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: generator cannot grant its own claim status; a configured separate model may apply actual semantic judgment. Default rule-only citation check is deterministic and could not alone warrant S3=A. - Claim being audited: the final agent-reported VERIFIED/REFUTED incident hypotheses and cited evidence supporting diagnosis/conclusion. - Ordinary reporting path: producing strategy&#39;s structured decision/hypotheses and summary, including its own claimed verification and cited refs. - Complementary access path: kernel independently resolves canonical refs to recorded tool observations, evidence and memory available in the run ledger; optional judge reads those observations under a distinct prompt and model identity. - Independence boundary: separate verifier code and optional judge model call from the producer; uses the same source-observation corpus (not direct independent re-probing of external environment), and is configurable/disableable by the operator. - Who acts on findings: kernel inserts hidden conclusionreview challenge to producer within backtrack budget; saved report/metrics also inform operator and profile quality gate. - Evidence: verifier contract/rules, separate judge, kernel corrective loop, default and optional app wiring. - Basis: implemented return-to-producer code and separate-model mode; no live independent review result witnessed. - Confidence: medium-high for code-level closure; stronger claims of verifier truthfulness are unsupported by testing here. - Caveats: canonical citation existence is not external truth, and a separately prompted judge sharing the same evidence corpus is only bounded independence. The repository contains no Go tests to independently corroborate runtime execution.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: operator-led prospective improvement of future incident-handling capability through frozen real-run datasets, candidate profile variants, offline evaluation, holdout gates, measured canary and return to active profile. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing external incident mix/service reliability and observed mismatch between current agent behavior and future target quality, failure rate, tool safety or cost. - Decisive decision or feedback right: owner/proposer chooses a candidate future profile version; non-inferiority gate compares candidate vs historical baseline and forbids failed promotion; a distinct approving human authorizes canary, the monitor auto-rolls back detected regressions, and owner chooses promotion. - Decision owner: legitimate local operator/approver for strategic option proposal/adoption; deterministic replay gate and canary monitor control test and rollback. No autonomous S4=A external trend strategist is claimed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dataset recording from actual runs, frozen holdout evaluation, profile candidate versioning, Gate thresholds, Approve anti-self-approval, percentage-bucketed canary rollout, control/canary stats and continuous rollback monitor. - Closure path: real incident-run outcomes/feedback become dataset cases; owner proposes candidate to improve future SRE responses; baseline/candidate replay produces gate evidence; different authorized approver accepts; routed future Runs exercise canary, with rollback on breach; successful owner promotion sets the new active profile version used by subsequent Runs. - Boundary reachability: internal/eval.Service implements all transitions, ResolveProfile routes real new Runs to canary/active versions, and API offers candidate/evaluation/approval/rollout operations; this is a supported human-parent improvement mode, not merely a README suggestion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an operator supplies the future option and authority to promote; automatic evaluation/rollback are supporting tests. P avoids assigning a strategic choice to the diagnostic LLM. - External distinction: incident outcomes and operator feedback originate from real monitored SRE services outside the harness&#39;s own model loop; frozen evaluation cases preserve these past external disturbances rather than generating synthetic internal tests alone. - Future / prospective distinction: a proposed candidate is assessed against a frozen holdout before it handles future incidents, and canary groups measure whether a prospective new configuration should become the next active operating default. - Adaptation option generated: a human operator/proposer supplies a new version of evolvable model, reasoning, memory or planner configuration, judged against holdout baseline and released only after a distinct human gate; there is no autonomous option inventor credited. - Path back into current capability / S3: StartCanary/ResolveProfile makes new Runs use the candidate profile percentage; Promote changes active profile for subsequent S1 Runs, while MonitorTick protects current S3 control by quarantining and rolling back regressions. - Evidence: real-run dataset recording, candidate replay/gate, separate human approval and canary, promotion and active routing, rollback monitor. - Basis: first-party persisted candidate/evaluation/canary implementation and return-to-operation controls. - Confidence: medium for supported human-guided future adaptation; no empirical dataset/canary deployment witnessed. - Caveats: dataset replay and regression monitoring do not independently sense geopolitical or strategic future. No model-origin selection of an outside future option is established. The README&#39;s phrase &quot;evolution loop is complete&quot; is read only as bounded product-level parent-guided capability improvement.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: operator-governed versioned policy/authority of the SRE agent profile, maintaining a non-relaxable risk floor and controlling when identity-critical tools/policies change across future Runs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: proposed efficiency/evolution changes that would weaken safety policy, permit destructive R3 actions or change an agent&#39;s enduring tool/authorization identity without legitimate authority. - Decisive decision or feedback right: human operator creates/edits immutable versioned profiles and must authorize frozen identity fields; evolution candidates cannot modify tools, approval policy, risk overrides, allowed aliases, strategy or flow; framework policy floor forbids R3 and requires approval for R2. - Decision owner: tenant administrator/legitimate human owner and enforced framework floor, not individual incident-solving agent or arbitrary extension plugin. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: profile.Spec frozen/evolvable distinction and ApplyPatch reject rules, policy.Guarded safety floor, identity-bound plan/action approvals, profile version pinning, tenant permissions and audit ledger. - Closure path: owner changes constitutive profile in permitted profile-creation/update pathway; a new immutable version persists, a Run pins the authorized version, and the worker&#39;s effective tool/policy scope is rebuilt under it. A candidate aiming to change a frozen field fails rather than silently changing the ultimate rules. - Boundary reachability: profile APIs and app are real entrypoints; profile.Spec is passed to worker tool/policy resolution, and the guard is composed around custom policy. The claimed P mode is a human-parent governing boundary, not autonomous S5=A. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ultimate identity/prohibition belongs to the operator/framework. The agent may optimize cognitive parameters but cannot authorize itself to relax safety-critical frozen fields. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: enduring agent&#39;s allowed tools, safety risk classes, approval requirements, strategy and immutable flow are constitution-level profile boundaries, distinct from approving an individual action intent. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: authorized human/operator plus non-bypassable safety floor; no independent agent&#39;s power to rewrite its own policy. - Return-to-operation path: new authorized profile version is persisted and pinned into subsequent Run creation; policy floor applies to every later tool intent. - Evidence: operator-owned profile and frozen fields, non-bypassable policy floor, human separate approval, profile API. - Basis: versioned schema, executable policy floor and owner-facing API code. - Confidence: medium-high for structural human authority and return, not for live compliance reliability. - Caveats: approving one risk-R2 command is S1 safety governance, not by itself S5. Only durable changes to the governing profile/identity plus non-relaxable floor support S5=P here.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9599f5d3766b568e88a3d0f18c8d1a7853518fb6/assessments/go/go-sre-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9599f5d3766b568e88a3d0f18c8d1a7853518fb6\">9599f5d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T21:40:17+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/bhandiwad/agent-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Pandita Agent Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/bhandiwad/agent-harness/tree/0827a48d5131537216e74cabd2acf78f0138686b\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 0827a48d5131537216e74cabd2acf78f0138686b\">0827a48 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-directed task execution and conversational/file/tool work with successive observations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User changes, variable model output, local file contents, tool errors, context and permission limits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The model chooses a tool name/arguments, delegates to an allowed specialist or returns a final answer; subsequent tool receipts can alter its next choice. - Decision owner: The configured assistant model, within operator-set tool schemas and permission bounds. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Durable session/turn history, PostgreSQL writer lock, budgets, installed adapters, native tools, separate approver and receipt validation. - Closure path: User turn → model generation → selected tool or final response → verified tool result/feedback appended → next agent model step or terminal answer. - Boundary reachability: The shipped Runner.tick and modelstep are wired from the installable worker and current config/agent.json gives the assistant selectable native file tools; a real provider endpoint must be configured by the operator. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the model leaves authorization and state transitions, but no discretionary interpretation of the user task or choice among tools; hence S1 choice is agent-owned. - Evidence: installed config, model/tool loop, dispatch. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: High for first-party wiring, not for untested live model behavior. - Caveats: Endpoint/keys and tenant authorization need real deployment; API limits and approval are external constraints, not autonomous owner rights.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No shipped closed attenuation relation between distinct concurrently operating S1 organizations is demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Potential conflicting specialist work or concurrent session writes; team handoff data and writer locking address different local hazards. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No distinct inter-S1 coordination actor decides a response to concrete peer conflict; fixed team order and single-session concurrency prevent local overlap. - Decision owner: Root S1 chooses permitted delegation; deterministic session code serializes handoffs. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Specialist team sequence, parent frames, Postgres writer lock and configured active-session capacity. - Closure path: Root task → specialist result → root context; no peer-interference observation → coordination judgment → revised independent S1 commitments loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Sequencing a hierarchy and blocking concurrent writes do not by themselves establish independent S1-unit oscillation regulation. - Evidence: team handoff/return, session ownership, storage. - Basis: structural bounded absence. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A tenant could build a fleet with external coordination; no S2-specific packaged correction relation was established here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No separately owned whole-system current-control function over a portfolio of autonomous operations is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Session concurrency, budget exhaustion, cancellation, uncertain external writes and approval delays. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Configuration/tenant owner sets limits and agent roster; runtime enforces caps and halts risky states, rather than autonomously revising whole-system resource/commitment priorities. - Decision owner: Operator/developer for configuration and human approver for action permission; model for its own S1 task steps. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: maxsteps/tokens/time, session locks, access-control checks, approval digest and reconciliation guard. - Closure path: Fixed limit violation → fail/pause/deny/reconcile; no evidenced autonomous or legitimate parent current-control bargaining and returned cross-unit decisions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the model leaves static limit enforcement, not a discretionary portfolio-control decision. Team delegation is task decomposition, not S3 by name. - Evidence: config and capacities, run limits/recovery, approval/receipt enforcement. - Basis: structural bounded absence. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A broader tenancy operation may have S3 outside this agent-service boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No default complementary, sufficiently independent audit of operational claims is wired into a corrective governance path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incorrect answers, unconfirmed tool side effects and fabricated completion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The optional completioncheck callback can reject format/content against a developer-supplied checker; tool receipts confirm transport outcomes, but no first-party separate reality-inspecting auditor makes an independent judgment. - Decision owner: Configured callback/adapter and deterministic runtime for validation; no S3 audit owner established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool result schema checks, write receipts, completion schema/check callbacks, test suite and event log. - Closure path: Model answer → optional direct callback → retry/fail, or tool invocation → receipt → model; this is routine completion/tool validation, not complementary audit access to independent operational reality. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An injected callback can be implemented by an external evaluator, but no shipped audit-specific independent actor/path is present or owned by a first-party standard mode. - Evidence: completion check and rejection, tool receipt validation. - Basis: structural bounded absence. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: An application can install an independent checker, but generic callback extensibility alone does not supply a first-party S3 mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No closed external-and-prospective adaptation cycle for future harness capability is installed. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing model providers, tools, user needs and knowledge; context windows and new requirements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: User/developer changes the model/tool configuration and prompts, while agent research occurs only within the current request. - Decision owner: Operator/developer for future capacity; same S1 model for present task response. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Configured model aliases, skills, context history/archive, provider protocol adapter and optional specialist handoff. - Closure path: Manual config changes can affect later execution; no product-owned future-environment sensing → adaptation option judgment → feedback into present S3/capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Context/history retention and task planning optimize current work; they do not authorize an agent to revise its organizational future capability. - Evidence: settings and agent contracts, context construction, installed config. - Basis: structural bounded absence. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Separate maintenance-agent examples do not automatically wire future adaptation into the deployed generic assistant.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party identity/ultimate-policy dispute resolution loop at the assessed agent-service recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Access violations, disallowed tools, untrusted input and action approval. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Tenant operator and external identity authority supply authorizations and policy; independent actor approves a particular write action, not ultimate organizational identity. - Decision owner: External operator/IdP/approver for fixed permissions; none for a packaged S5 function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: RS256 identity verification, role permissions, immutable configuration digest, approval digest and untrusted-input isolation. - Closure path: Token/policy match → permitted/denied session/tool; action approval returns one tool gate, not an identity-level dispute → legitimate authority decision → enduring operating-policy return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agent instructions explicitly treat tool output as data and cannot change policy authority. Human approval is narrow execution consent, not S5. - Evidence: authorization and config digest, approval logic, policy/identity config. - Basis: structural bounded absence. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: A separate operating institution may exercise S5 outside the declared product boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ce4965715a8d34e1ca0c4292be0cfa66387ff968/assessments/pa/pandita-agent-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ce4965715a8d34e1ca0c4292be0cfa66387ff968\">ce49657 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T20:45:47+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/cUDGk/nipus-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">nipus-harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/cUDGk/nipus-harness/tree/0c0349ee7f37d9bc0334c1d5ea7802c49f8e950b\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 0c0349ee7f37d9bc0334c1d5ea7802c49f8e950b\">0c0349e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: complete bounded coding/research work and correct it against actual tool/acceptance feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown source, failed checks, scope violations, incomplete answers and transient model/tool errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose inspections, edits, commands and repair strategy; declare finish or an evidenced blocker. - Decision owner: model worker instantiated by the first-party prompt/tool/continuation composition; optional declared child owns its bounded local work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Pi session/model library, owned tools, boxing, request gates, acceptance runner, result/evidence/diff validation, persisted sessions and ledger. - Closure path: goal → tool-backed worker → finish → real checks/evidence/scope → reasons returned to same worker → revised work or honest terminal result. - Boundary reachability: CLI createSession wires the owned customTools and prompt, then directly invokes runLoop; dependency execution does not replace this composition. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the worker leaves checks/locks but no task-specific implementation or correction decisions. - Evidence: src/cli.ts; src/loop.ts; src/tools.ts; src/prompt.ts; src/session.ts; src/delegate.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for source wiring; not an empirical outcome claim. - Caveats: Source reachability only; Windows/model prerequisites were not exercised. A mock provider is not credited as autonomous.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: prevent destructive overlap among independent task-worker runs sharing folders and host jobs. - Distinct S1 units: separate CLI task/worker runs with their own goal, session and result on the same host. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent writers in an identical or nested workspace undo each other&#39;s edits; overlapping Unity/heavy processes contend for an exclusive host resource; crashed owners may leave orphan processes. - Attenuating coordination relation: CLI lockWorkspace uses a short atomic table lock plus real-path exact/parent-child checks before acquiring a writer lock; hostJob waits on named owner-aware locks, detects a busy Unity project and reaps stale-owner processes before reuse. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: workspacebusy prevents a second writer from starting; busy host jobs queue until safe or return busy at timeout into worker/acceptance feedback. The lock is released after the owning task/process ends. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the relations identify actual workspace/resource collision domains and modify whether/when another independent operational run may execute. - Disturbance / variety regulated: cross-run edit interference and exclusive host-process conflict. - Decisive decision or feedback right: admit, defer or reject conflicting runs under the fixed collision policy. - Decision owner: developer-authored lock/admission policy and caller-declared host lock identity; autonomous organizational coordination discretion is not composed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: exclusive-create lock files, owner PID/start-time checks, workspace table, queue timeout, orphan cleanup and returned status. - Closure path: another active owner&#39;s collision → refusal/wait → subsequent run starts only after safe release or receives busy evidence. - Boundary reachability: src/cli.ts invokes lockWorkspace before work and releases it in finally; src/host.ts hostJob is reachable through declared tools and acceptance jobs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: specific coordination machinery is shipped, but its response is predetermined rather than chosen/revised by an autonomous coordinator. - Evidence: src/host.ts; src/cli.ts; src/tools.ts; tests/parallel.test.ts; README.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for source wiring; not an empirical outcome claim. - Caveats: C does not claim coordination across hosts/installations or semantic negotiation. Tests were inspected as witnesses, not executed here.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: whole-system inside-and-now control - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate variety and checked alternatives are recorded below. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying function-specific path established. - Decision owner: ordinary operational agent, developer and caller rights do not establish this distinct function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: src/gate.ts, src/commands.ts, src/cli.ts, src/delegate.ts - Closure path: Per-role/run quotas, account-limit enforcement, static tool/model profiles and host locks bound tasks. status --active exposes state and stop/resume provides local lifecycle intervention, but no shipped whole-system commitment/resource decision with a distinct supervisory owner and return contract is established. Generic caller control is not automatically parent S3. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: positive and supported parent alternatives were tested before this bounded absence conclusion. - Evidence: src/gate.ts; src/commands.ts; src/cli.ts; src/delegate.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for source wiring; not an empirical outcome claim. - Caveats: Bounded to the reviewed supported distribution; custom future extensions are not evidence of a shipped path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: complementary independent audit - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate variety and checked alternatives are recorded below. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying function-specific path established. - Decision owner: ordinary operational agent, developer and caller rights do not establish this distinct function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: src/loop.ts, src/rules.ts, src/delegate.ts, tests/review.test.ts - Closure path: runLoop checks acceptance, evidence and diffs as the normal completion path and sends failures back to the same worker. Suspect violations and needsjudgment do not instantiate an independent source auditor. Arbitrary declared roles can be composed into review but no audit-specific independent claim/judgment/corrective loop is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: positive and supported parent alternatives were tested before this bounded absence conclusion. - Evidence: src/loop.ts; src/rules.ts; src/delegate.ts; tests/review.test.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for source wiring; not an empirical outcome claim. - Caveats: Bounded to the reviewed supported distribution; custom future extensions are not evidence of a shipped path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: external and prospective capability adaptation - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate variety and checked alternatives are recorded below. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying function-specific path established. - Decision owner: ordinary operational agent, developer and caller rights do not establish this distinct function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: src/cli.ts, src/session.ts, src/loop.ts, src/prompt.ts - Closure path: Web retrieval serves the current research goal; session persistence, retry and resume retain ongoing work. No supported outside-and-then option-development/adoption path changes the worker&#39;s future repertoire; task/model configuration edits are inputs, not a closed parent adaptation function. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: positive and supported parent alternatives were tested before this bounded absence conclusion. - Evidence: src/cli.ts; src/session.ts; src/loop.ts; src/prompt.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for source wiring; not an empirical outcome claim. - Caveats: Bounded to the reviewed supported distribution; custom future extensions are not evidence of a shipped path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: identity and ultimate-policy closure - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate variety and checked alternatives are recorded below. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying function-specific path established. - Decision owner: ordinary operational agent, developer and caller rights do not establish this distinct function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: src/prompt.ts, src/cli.ts, src/gate.ts, src/loop.ts - Closure path: Fixed goals, task scope, forbidden terms, model/tool gates and clean-room declarations constrain operational execution. A blocked/needsjudgment result requests task-owner judgment, but no explicit ultimate identity/policy matter and authoritative return into standing governance is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: positive and supported parent alternatives were tested before this bounded absence conclusion. - Evidence: src/prompt.ts; src/cli.ts; src/gate.ts; src/loop.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for source wiring; not an empirical outcome claim. - Caveats: Bounded to the reviewed supported distribution; custom future extensions are not evidence of a shipped path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/83ba5c0624e2056ca6c034332624e473993f2f01/assessments/ni/nipus-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 83ba5c0624e2056ca6c034332624e473993f2f01\">83ba5c0 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T21:52:07+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/mnbresearch/crewdeck\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CrewDeck</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/mnbresearch/crewdeck/tree/7925ffb4c5e922f0081cce9ca59f62190b6e7864\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7925ffb4c5e922f0081cce9ca59f62190b6e7864\">7925ffb &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: configured autonomous participant agents execute assigned coding/research/other tasks and return results into the company portfolio. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task requirements, tool outputs, errors and changing local work conditions faced by each participating agent. - Decisive decision or feedback right: each invoked autonomous CLI/HTTP worker chooses how to perform its assigned task and returns an outcome; CrewDeck decides neither the model&#39;s work steps nor its answer. - Decision owner: the configured external agent actor for task-local S1 discretion; human/manual and simulation modes do not supply this autonomous state. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: nextTaskFor, claim, context prompt assembly, shell/HTTP/remote adapters, run outputs, status transitions and persisted state. - Closure path: operator creates an assigned task → scheduler/remote claim supplies context to the AI agent → agent works and reports → completeRun records the outcome and result/cost → later tasks consume the result or the same work is retried/approved. - Boundary reachability: shell/HTTP agent adapters are shipped and directly called by the real server tick; remote participants have a shipped claim/complete protocol. The documented CLI examples require user-supplied providers and do not imply internal provider capabilities are CrewDeck&#39;s own metasystem. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the external agent makes meaningful task-specific execution decisions in the supported assembled operating mode; CrewDeck&#39;s deterministic queue/rules do not acquire that discretion. - Evidence: README launch and agent adapter modes, server dispatch and remote claim/complete, runner implementation. - Basis: structural, supported configuration and first-party wiring. - Confidence: medium-high for reachable assembled agent execution; no live proprietary provider run was performed in this review. - Caveats: neither browser sim nor a manual user task demonstrates agent ownership, and no foreign agent&#39;s S2–S5 is inherited.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: explicit ownership/lock and dependency guards attenuate competing execution and premature consumption of work across distinct participating agents. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two agent workers claiming the same task/agent capacity concurrently and downstream work starting before its prerequisite result is available. - Decisive decision or feedback right: assigned task identity and dependency choice originate in portfolio configuration; deterministic first-party claim eligibility denies incompatible concurrent starts and releases locks on completion/timeout. - Decision owner: operator/configuration for assignment/dependency intent and deterministic runtime for fixed admission; there is no autonomous agent selecting/revising the inter-agent coordination policy at this boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-task run lock with expiry, one-task-per-agent invariant, blockedBy, cycle checks and status/attempt state. - Closure path: candidate agent run is tested against assignment, live locks and completed prerequisites → conflicting claim is rejected or deferred → a completed/expired run releases the lock and changes subsequent eligibility. - Boundary reachability: the same claim and nextTaskFor invariants are called in real server dispatch and remote polling and are exercised by first-party engine tests. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the anti-contention path is function-specific and operational, but its decisive policy is configured/deterministic. An autonomous coordinating owner would require further composition; generic agent-to-agent conversation is not claimed. - Distinct S1 units: independently configured shell/HTTP/remote participants performing different assigned tasks at the company recursion. - Inter-S1 disturbance: competing workers could otherwise double-execute a shared work item, or a consumer agent could act on incomplete producer output. - Attenuating coordination relation: exclusive task/worker claims plus explicit completed-prerequisite gating and dependency-cycle rejection. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: denied claims remain unstarted; completion or timeout changes eligibility; dependent agents receive completed producer summaries only once prerequisite tasks reach done. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the positive witness is the source-enforced concurrent-claim conflict and consumption-of-incomplete-output prevention, not the mere existence of task ordering or a board. - Evidence: selection, claim, completion, lock expiry, claimed-node tests, real server dispatch. - Basis: structural first-party code and passing offline tests. - Confidence: medium; the lock conflict is direct, whereas broader semantic work-product interference is not resolved by this mechanism. - Caveats: no independent agent negotiates shared files or autonomously revises coordination policy.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: the company operator reviews whole-portfolio current tasks, staff capacity and spend and sets effective resource, priority and commitment constraints. - Disturbance / variety regulated: competing work, agent availability, constrained cost and tasks that fail or await review. - Decisive decision or feedback right: human operator creates/reassigns/prioritizes tasks, changes budgets/pauses and approves or requests rework across the company portfolio. - Decision owner: legitimate installed company owner/operator; the server merely enforces selected rules and makes current state visible. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: /api/state and /api/summary, UI and editing API, budget accounting and hard dispatch stop, task review and status ledger. - Closure path: operator inspects shared current state → changes assigned worker, task priority or monthly budget, pauses an agent or rejects a reviewed task → saved state and next dispatcher tick govern subsequent commitments and execution. - Boundary reachability: the shipped self-hosted API exposes whole-company state and operator mutations that the same production dispatch path immediately consumes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an AI agent may perform an assigned task, but no shipped agent owns company-wide current prioritization or budget selection; numerical sorting and hard stops enforce human settings. - Whole-system current view: summary spans task statuses, active agents, total current spend and waiting reviews, with full state available to the board. - Current-control decision scope: organization-wide worker availability, priority, task assignment and cost envelopes, plus returned acceptance/rework control. - Evidence: summary and controls, budget/review gates, operating API. - Basis: explicit operator mode and structural return-to-dispatch. - Confidence: high for installed human current-control path. - Caveats: a human&#39;s acceptance of an individual deliverable alone is not S3; the positive mapping relies on the broader current portfolio view and cross-agent authority.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no first-party complementary independent audit loop over operational truth is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false self-reported completion or faulty work would require evidence outside the ordinary participant result and routine operator approval. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not supplied as a distinct audit judgment; generic human approve/reject of the returned output is task acceptance. - Decision owner: no separate first-party auditor identified. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ordinary result comments, run log, review state and operator approval. - Closure path: no complementary independently sourced findings-to-current-control path supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: recordkeeping and task acceptance do not create independence from the agent&#39;s own report. - Evidence: completion and approval flow, adapter-reported result. - Basis: structural bounded negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a separately configured reviewer agent could be given a task, but no shipped independent evidence path is wired as a company-level audit loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party externally/prospectively coupled capability-adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: market, user, model/tool or other future change is not sensed and turned into a runtime capability change by CrewDeck. - Decisive decision or feedback right: absent; humans can edit the portfolio and agents can research as task work, but no shipped S4 feedback path selects future adaptations. - Decision owner: not established at the company boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: goals, routine task generation, company mission field and task comments. - Closure path: no separate outside/future model → adaptation proposal → current capability revision path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ordinary planning and scheduled repetition do not establish S4. - Evidence: goal/task construction and routines, routine tick. - Basis: structural bounded negative. - Confidence: high for inspected shipped composition. - Caveats: future adaptation could be implemented by external bots through generic APIs; such a hypothetical composition is not present evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity/ultimate-policy disagreement or exceptional matter is routed to legitimate final authority and returned as a governing resolution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tension over company mission, ethos or final operating constraints is not handled by a separate runtime identity-policy loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S5-level closed path; the human directly edits company name/mission and ordinary runtime settings. - Decision owner: human configuration authority exists, but no evidenced first-party S5 decision transaction is supplied. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: company mission string in state/prompt, human API settings and approval/budget controls. - Closure path: static mission is passed into future task prompts; no surfaced identity matter → legitimate ultimate decision → returned resolution loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a mission string and task approvals are not an authoritative identity/policy decision process. - Evidence: company state and prompt, mission ingestion, company API and operator controls. - Basis: structural bounded negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the host human can always change values out-of-band, but this does not itself establish first-party S5 closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/85f36bfe535935b4130e12aef09c7644c3e53854/assessments/cr/crewdeck.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 85f36bfe535935b4130e12aef09c7644c3e53854\">85f36bf &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T18:28:14+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/aa1143/NamiDSH\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NamiDSH</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/aa1143/NamiDSH/tree/629e9efcb121b2805a2f3c48565144b732c0ff7f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 629e9efcb121b2805a2f3c48565144b732c0ff7f\">629e9ef &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: A self-hosted model-driven session performs user-requested observation/tool actions and produces a revised answer to task/environment feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User tasks, changing available tools, local time/filesystem observations, returned tool values/errors and model-selected next steps. - Decisive decision or feedback right: In DeepSeek mode, the model determines assistant text or tool name/arguments from its session history and current advertised tool schemas; after receipts it can choose the next action or end. - Decision owner: Configured agent model through the shipped first-party agentLoop/DeepSeek adapter path; tool executor and host transport enforce choices without choosing the task strategy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: maxSteps=8 default bound, tool registration/list, JSON parsing and errors, pre-execute waterfall, event stream, abort signal, in-memory session log and restricted filesystem tool. - Closure path: User input → first-party webserver/session/loop → configured model tool decision → first-party ToolRuntime/local tool → logged result → next model turn/answer → web SSE response. - Boundary reachability: apps/web/src/main.ts actually installs spine, model adapter and webserver; the AgentLoop.run reached by HTTP executes the model/tool feedback loop with the shipped read-only FS/time tools. This is a supported opt-in real provider mode, not merely an external model callback skeleton. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The agent chooses tool actions and final responses in configured DeepSeek mode. Deterministic mock fallback, model provider infrastructure and safety hooks do not themselves own that discretion. - Evidence: entry wiring, agent loop, DeepSeek SSE tool-call adapter, tools, session. - Basis: Explicit installed real-provider mode and first-party closed tool-result feedback; offline tests corroborate routing only. - Confidence: High for source reachability; no live paid DeepSeek E2E attempted. - Caveats: Mock mode is deterministic and all first-party file tools are read-only; neither implies broader system authority or external-provider correctness.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No coordination of multiple independently autonomous S1 work units with a specific attenuated interaction conflict is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Plugin service name conflicts and simultaneous web sessions can occur, but these are registry/transport concerns rather than demonstrated inter-S1 organizational interference. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Cordis resolves service registration and dependency readiness; sessions remain independent records with no reciprocal task coordination decisions. - Decision owner: Deterministic registry/fiber scheduler and the operator who composes plugins. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Service uniqueness checks, plugin hot-unplug lifecycle, event channels and per-session SSE filters. - Closure path: Service availability changes reload dependent plugin implementations, not a feedback relation between two task-operating S1 cells. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A model sees its own session and tools, not conflicts between separate autonomous operational units. - Evidence: spine, tool registry, webserver, agent tests. - Basis: Bounded negative against shipped multi-session and plugin-interference candidates. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Future compositions could introduce many autonomous agents, but such a mode is not shipped.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No whole-system current controller regulates a portfolio of autonomous S1 work, priorities and resource commitments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Individual session turn limits, client disconnects, tool errors and plugin availability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The model performs local S1 continuation; the host enforces fixed maxSteps and client cancellation. No second actor holds cross-session budget/resource/commitment discretion. - Decision owner: Local agent for its task choices; deterministic runtime and human operator for installed configuration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: maxSteps counter, AbortController, dependency readiness, session event log and tool pre-execute hooks. - Closure path: Limit/abort terminates or prevents one run; no whole-portfolio observation → S3 priority/resource decision → changed subsequent multi-unit operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Stop enforcement and tool transport do not own whole-system current management merely because they can block an action. - Evidence: loop, webserver, tools. - Basis: Bounded negative. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: A host could add an operator management plugin; generic plugin hooks do not constitute an installed S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent complementary audit pathway checks the actual agent&#39;s claims through alternate reality evidence and returns findings into control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool action mistakes and stale results can be tested or blocked, but product does not install a separate independent truth-checking auditor. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Optional pre-execute hooks can block action and unit tests verify mock behavior, neither produces an independent audit judgment from distinct operational evidence. - Decision owner: Operator-authored guard/test runner outside the packaged runtime; no S3 reviewer in standard deployed mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool-result events, session logs, mocked agent tests and mock/real Cordis compatibility comparison. - Closure path: Direct tool result → same agent context; separate test/guard → local failure/block. No independent audit finding → accountable S3 corrective change. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Technical testing and configurable permission hooks are not independent S3 just because a different function handles them. - Evidence: tool hooks, agent test guard, compatibility test. - Basis: Bounded negative. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Operator may externally audit runs; not a first-party packaged complementary audit channel.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No external-and-prospective strategy/adaptation feedback loop is installed in the session&#39;s first-party distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New tasks, plugin/provider availability, workspace changes and future model upgrades. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operator may replace plugins/provider or edit persona; the agent plans only the present chat turn and follows current tool results. - Decision owner: Operator/developer for longer-term model/tool changes; no strategically deciding agent in the standard distribution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Dynamic plugin dependency restart, configurable provider/model, ephemeral session log and prompt service. - Closure path: Current task → local reply, or manual plugin/config update → next run; no external forecast/adaptation option → S3 conversation → future capability change loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A hot-swappable model service is an adaptation mechanism, not evidence that the agent models changing external capability and selects revisions. - Evidence: entry, spine, agent loop, plugin tests. - Basis: Bounded negative. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Educational articles explain extensibility, not installed strategic decision ownership.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party identity/ultimate-policy deliberation and returned mandate operates at the local harness recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Access to local file tools, persona changes, model credentials and user preferences. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operator preselects workspace, model and persona and may compose a permission guard; the shipped web agent lacks a channel for identity-level matters to a legitimate parent authority with returned policy. - Decision owner: Human developer/operator outside the agent session; static code and optional plugin veto enforce limits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Static system prompt, root-scoped tools, runtime configuration, dependency hooks and tool waterfall. - Closure path: Ordinary guard veto or user input influences one run; no identity/policy issue → ultimate authority → authoritative decision → subsequent harness governance feedback. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Persona text, workspace permissions and generic approval-like hooks do not transfer the S5 decision right to the agent or establish a parent-governed closed mode. - Evidence: system prompt, entry, tools. - Basis: Bounded negative. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Maintainer governance of the public project is a separate organization, not the installed local session.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/39aef6e78dd1ce2ff1a6b9a9b9fe4cba8b0f2ac2/assessments/na/namidsh.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 39aef6e78dd1ce2ff1a6b9a9b9fe4cba8b0f2ac2\">39aef6e &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T20:24:14+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ketiyohannes/mint\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mint</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ketiyohannes/mint/tree/0f98e1b4a17f9b73aabc6f8789e604d5902d648d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 0f98e1b4a17f9b73aabc6f8789e604d5902d648d\">0f98e1b &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce context-sensitive tutoring, exercises and judgments of demonstrated understanding. - Disturbance / variety regulated: different materials, questions, wrong/partial learner answers and requests for additional help. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose grounded explanation/practice content and judge an answer against materials; propose a curriculum or session wrap within the teaching contract. - Decision owner: model-backed tutor instantiated by the first-party job composition; learner owns goals and acceptance, code owns state transitions and safety limits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SDK/app-server transport, folder/domain hooks, schemas and retry, topic-progress rules, JSON store, UI and exercise sandbox. - Closure path: learner answer → judgeCheck → validated verdict/feedback → submitCheck stores attempts and updates progress; next session/thread consumes refreshed context. tutorReply returns a grounded response into the continuing thread. - Boundary reachability: shipped ChatService/SessionService invoke the owned jobs through Kernel.runtime; the Claude/Codex adapters provide the configured execution seam. No development agent is borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the model actor, transport, allowlists and progress rules cannot choose the same explanations or misconception verdicts; Demo is explicitly scripted and is not the credited mode. - Evidence: src/main/jobs/index.ts; src/main/services/sessions.ts; src/main/services/threads.ts; src/main/agents/claude.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for implemented ownership/wiring; no claim of learning efficacy. - Caveats: provider access and a real model are required; learner activity drives the sessions and accepted records.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party path for interference among independent S1 tutoring units at the declared boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the candidate distinction is interference among independent S1 tutoring units; observed nearby mechanisms are assessed below. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive right with the required closure established. - Decision owner: no qualifying owner identified for this function; learner/model/developer ownership of ordinary teaching remains as described. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: src/main/services/setup.ts, src/main/services/threads.ts, src/main/services/sessions.ts. - Closure path: Concurrent class planning, per-class locks and fresh-state merges prevent duplicate jobs or lost updates, but the reviewed cell exposes one tutor through several service facets. No specific inter-S1 mutual-adjustment relation is established by these storage primitives. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no positive functional witness is supplied by the inspected alternatives, so agent/constructor/parent ownership is not inferred. - Evidence: src/main/services/setup.ts; src/main/services/threads.ts; src/main/services/sessions.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high, bounded to the supplied distribution. - Caveats: custom integrations or a wider institution could add this function; that possibility is not evidence of a shipped path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party path for whole-system resource/commitment control at the declared boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the candidate distinction is whole-system resource/commitment control; observed nearby mechanisms are assessed below. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive right with the required closure established. - Decision owner: no qualifying owner identified for this function; learner/model/developer ownership of ordinary teaching remains as described. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: src/main/learning/engine.ts, src/main/services/setup.ts, src/main/agents/claude.ts. - Closure path: Curriculum selection, lesson progress and help-level changes are the primary tutoring transformation. Cancellation, fixed turn limits and a learner selecting a model do not constitute whole-system current resource bargaining or intervention. The inspected supported learner mode supplies no separate function-specific parent S3 loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no positive functional witness is supplied by the inspected alternatives, so agent/constructor/parent ownership is not inferred. - Evidence: src/main/learning/engine.ts; src/main/services/setup.ts; src/main/agents/claude.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high, bounded to the supplied distribution. - Caveats: custom integrations or a wider institution could add this function; that possibility is not evidence of a shipped path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party path for independent challenge to operational tutoring claims at the declared boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the candidate distinction is independent challenge to operational tutoring claims; observed nearby mechanisms are assessed below. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive right with the required closure established. - Decision owner: no qualifying owner identified for this function; learner/model/developer ownership of ordinary teaching remains as described. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: src/main/jobs/index.ts, src/main/services/sessions.ts, src/main/jobs/run.ts. - Closure path: The model judges the learner&#39;s answer, not a tutor&#39;s ordinary claim of successful work. Diagram repair, schema validation and learner-code tests are routine production checks. No independent direct-evidence challenge of the tutoring cell with a returned corrective control path is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no positive functional witness is supplied by the inspected alternatives, so agent/constructor/parent ownership is not inferred. - Evidence: src/main/jobs/index.ts; src/main/services/sessions.ts; src/main/jobs/run.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high, bounded to the supplied distribution. - Caveats: custom integrations or a wider institution could add this function; that possibility is not evidence of a shipped path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party path for external and prospective adaptation of the tutoring system&#39;s capability at the declared boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the candidate distinction is external and prospective adaptation of the tutoring system&#39;s capability; observed nearby mechanisms are assessed below. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive right with the required closure established. - Decision owner: no qualifying owner identified for this function; learner/model/developer ownership of ordinary teaching remains as described. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: src/main/services/setup.ts, src/main/jobs/index.ts, src/main/learning/engine.ts. - Closure path: Source import, web-assisted answers, curriculum re-planning and learner-independence history inform delivery of the current class. They do not develop and return options for changing this harness&#39;s future operational capability. No separate supported autonomous or parent adaptation loop is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no positive functional witness is supplied by the inspected alternatives, so agent/constructor/parent ownership is not inferred. - Evidence: src/main/services/setup.ts; src/main/jobs/index.ts; src/main/learning/engine.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high, bounded to the supplied distribution. - Caveats: custom integrations or a wider institution could add this function; that possibility is not evidence of a shipped path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party path for identity or ultimate-policy tension at the tutoring-cell boundary at the declared boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the candidate distinction is identity or ultimate-policy tension at the tutoring-cell boundary; observed nearby mechanisms are assessed below. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive right with the required closure established. - Decision owner: no qualifying owner identified for this function; learner/model/developer ownership of ordinary teaching remains as described. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: src/main/services/setup.ts, src/main/services/chat.ts, src/main/services/sessions.ts. - Closure path: Mission fields, fixed pedagogy, trusted domains and record/source acceptance constrain the teaching task. Changing a class goal or approving a source/record is ordinary operational input. No path frames an unresolved identity/ultimate-policy matter, routes it to a legitimate ultimate authority, and returns that decision to govern the cell. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no positive functional witness is supplied by the inspected alternatives, so agent/constructor/parent ownership is not inferred. - Evidence: src/main/services/setup.ts; src/main/services/chat.ts; src/main/services/sessions.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high, bounded to the supplied distribution. - Caveats: custom integrations or a wider institution could add this function; that possibility is not evidence of a shipped path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/bff005c3dfa3f2e68b435916ea922264b1bacad1/assessments/mi/mint.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit bff005c3dfa3f2e68b435916ea922264b1bacad1\">bff005c &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T22:09:18+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/evolution301/agent-control-tower\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">agent-control-tower</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/evolution301/agent-control-tower/tree/14c6c98aa3ab741a5fe42afe10021d3b16deeb4d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 14c6c98aa3ab741a5fe42afe10021d3b16deeb4d\">14c6c98 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: configured role model performs a delegated task with context and workspace tools, reporting the resulting work product. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ambiguous brief, changing code/file contents and command feedback encountered within a role assignment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the first-party OpenAI-compatible model loop selects tool calls and the next work step based on returned outputs, within a configured step budget. - Decision owner: role model in the supported OpenAI-compatible adapter; externally configured CLI/DSH agents also have task-local discretion, but their private internal autonomy is not imputed to the tower. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: role prompt with mandate/write scopes, model-chat tool loop, shell execution, task assignment, artifacts and report intake. - Closure path: operator dispatches work → role model requests tool calls → host returns workspace observations → model adapts tool calls and returns a result → board records the report and the operator may accept or rework. - Boundary reachability: dispatch selects the shipped openai adapter and calls its own model-to-runshell loop; it is executable with a user-supplied compatible API endpoint and key, not merely an abstract pluggable interface. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model chooses the substantive implementation steps; neither the CLI nor the operator predetermines each tool call. - Evidence: adapter model/tool loop; dispatch integration; README modes. - Basis: first-party control flow in a supported installed mode. - Confidence: high for reachable decision structure; no paid model execution claimed. - Caveats: mock-mode deterministic completion is not S1 evidence, and the shell tool is a host-enforced mechanism rather than an S1 decision owner.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no materially closed, function-specific feedback relation regulating disturbances between distinct S1 units was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: potential collisions when roles edit the same workspace and receive overlapping instructions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: role assignments and write-scope advice are configured by the operator; the CLI iterates a static batch and never detects or resolves competing edits across S1 agents. - Decision owner: no first-party S2 owner shown for inter-S1 disturbance feedback. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-role workspace paths, instruction-level write scopes, optional batch spacing and task-key idempotency. - Closure path: dispatch routes tasks to roles and records status; no observed conflict-feedback → changed inter-role behavior relation is implemented. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: routing, sequential iteration, distinct directories and prompts alone do not evidence autonomous/constructed S2 coordination. - Evidence: batch loop and serial gap; prompt write-scope text; role/task states. - Basis: bounded structural negative, not a statement that coordinated external agent teams are impossible. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: configured external DSH sessions may coordinate themselves, but their private inter-agent control is outside this boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: a human lead supervises the entire local work portfolio and intervenes in role commitments, acceptance and rework. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfinished tasks, failures, evidence gaps and attention overload across active roles. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operator chooses role and brief, changes the role roster, sees all task statuses and attention, accepts or sends work back for another attempt. - Decision owner: local lead/operator in the documented self-hosted mode; not the model running a delegated S1 task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: full board/snapshot, attention list, inbox, audit ledger, role-add/remove and accept/rework commands. - Closure path: operator inspects whole-board/attention state → assigns new work or selects rework, acceptance and role setup → mutated task/role state governs next dispatch and future capacity allocation. - Boundary reachability: the same CLI exposes both a complete task-role view and actionable role/dispatch/rework commands against the same persisted board used by actual adapters. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the lead owns cross-role current commitments; transitions, status views and retry counters are deterministic supporting mechanics, not autonomous resource decisions. - Whole-system current view: buildsnapshot/tower board aggregates role states, tasks in flight and attention across the workspace, not just an individual report. - Current-control decision scope: selection/assignment of current work to roles, who remains active on the roster, acceptance of commitments and intervention on rework/escalation. - Evidence: board and attention commands; dispatch/accept/rework; role roster commands; board task state. - Basis: complete first-party operator control loop. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: human acceptance alone would not be S3; the claim also requires the whole-role view and operational role/assignment changes. The dashboard is read-only; decisions return through CLI.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: a separately invoked verification path checks report claims against actual workspace artifacts and configured executable commands before acceptance. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false positive claims that files exist or checks passed when no underlying artifact/output supports them. - Decisive decision or feedback right: configured verifier requirements select mandatory report evidence, filesystem checks and optional real commands; the operator invokes acceptance and determines subsequent rework. - Decision owner: first-party deterministic verification constructor plus explicit human acceptance owner; no independently deciding AI auditor is bundled. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: verifyreport, file existence checks, subprocess command execution, structured findings and mandatory rejection-to-rework path. - Closure path: agent submits ordinary markdown report → accept invokes verifier&#39;s workspace filesystem and real commands → failed independent observations produce problems → CLI transitions to rework; passing checks allow accepted → affected task&#39;s next execution/acceptance changes. - Boundary reachability: verification is called directly in shipped cmdaccept and cmddispatch; optional verifycommands are supported configuration, with non-optional file-existence checks always executed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: complementary observations are independent of the report text but the checks are deterministic/configured and the ultimate decision is human; hence constructor-mode C, not autonomous A. - Claim being audited: a role&#39;s report that an artifact exists and specified checks were performed and succeeded. - Ordinary reporting path: parsereport ingests model-authored sections (Files, Checks, Summary, Status) into task/report and inbox. - Complementary access path: verifier reads actual workspace paths and executes configured shell verification commands with return codes and recorded output tails. - Independence boundary: filesystem and command exit codes do not rely on the model&#39;s natural-language claim; however same workspace is writable by the agent and config may omit executable checks, so this is limited evidence of artifact existence/command results, not independent work-quality certification. - Who acts on findings: cmdaccept refuses transition to accepted and puts the task into rework; operator can re-dispatch under configured round limit. - Evidence: verification implementation; accept and rework control; reported-content parsing. - Basis: real alternate evidence path and mandatory gate; constructor classification. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: a malicious role could manipulate shared files, and the verifier does not independently understand semantic correctness; stronger audit requires configured commands or an independent reviewer with separate trust boundary.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no shipped loop senses external/future changes and revises the tower&#39;s capability or operating model as an adaptation decision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future environmental change is not an independent monitored input into tower adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: missing; an operator can modify role configuration and agents can be asked to research, but no built-in future/environment intelligence owner returns adopted changes. - Decision owner: no S4 actor in the assessed tower. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: manually edited roles/config, briefs, history and optional external model work. - Closure path: no first-party external/prospective sensing → adaptive option → decided capability change loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-generated suggestions within a task and generic ability to edit configuration do not establish a returned future-directed adaptation function. - Evidence: configuration; role setup and manual CLI changes; architecture. - Basis: bounded source negative. - Confidence: high for inspected first-party behavior. - Caveats: a separately created strategy role is theoretically possible but not instantiated as S4 closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no function-specific ultimate-policy/identity conflict resolution loop with legitimate authority and return is furnished. - Disturbance / variety regulated: competing ultimate mandates or exceptional organizational policy issues are not represented as a distinct operating transaction. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operator supplies briefs and role policies in configuration, but no S5 question-to-final-authority-and-back path is established. - Decision owner: operator controls the config outside any demonstrated S5 procedure. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: static role descriptions, mandate metadata (requester, strength, policy) and user-issued task commands. - Closure path: ordinary task mandates are persisted and read during execution; no ultimate-policy issue → adjudication → authoritative resolution → governed future operation loop is implemented. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: passing an operator policy string to a role and enforcing task-specific rounds is not deciding collective identity. - Evidence: mandate creation; board mandate storage; role configuration. - Basis: bounded structural negative. - Confidence: high for installed tower. - Caveats: the human operator has ultimate out-of-band control of a local installation, but authority alone does not prove a first-party S5 feedback loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/b79a4a0daf889bd8c1990c95d0a989217dbd2e12/assessments/ag/agent-control-tower.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit b79a4a0daf889bd8c1990c95d0a989217dbd2e12\">b79a4a0 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-11T20:11:16+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/cuijiejie/open-agent-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open Agent Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-11\">2026-11-11</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/cuijiejie/open-agent-harness/tree/9673ebb1ab77e89042a95e6c64cf19e2e2794943\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 9673ebb1ab77e89042a95e6c64cf19e2e2794943\">9673ebb &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: interpret variable user work, choose appropriate tools/actions, incorporate feedback and return a task result. - Disturbance / variety regulated: natural-language tasks, discovered file/tool state, tool errors, model uncertainty and interrupted sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the configured model chooses a next tool invocation or response within a DeepAgents turn based on returned messages and state. - Decision owner: model executing through the installed DeepAgents graph; first-party platform chooses configured tools and constraints but does not decide ordinary semantic task steps. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: user-scoped OpenShell backend, MCP/skill tool registry, role filtering, middleware, checkpointer, SSE transport and cache. - Closure path: authenticated chat request → createagent → model/tool cycle → streamed tool observations and updated checkpoint → subsequent model action/final user response. - Boundary reachability: src/chat/stream.py calls the first-party createagent; its AgentManager constructs createdeepagent with the configured model and tool suite, serving live sessions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: discretionary tool selection is model-owned; password, cache, sandbox and RBAC mechanics are deterministic support. - Evidence: agent construction, streaming execution. - Basis: structural; confidence: high for packaged configured-model mode. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: source-static review; no live model/provider benchmark or performance inference.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no evidenced inter-S1 collision attenuation with a dedicated coordination relation and returned behavior for distinct operational agents. - Disturbance / variety regulated: users can use parallel sessions and isolated sandboxes, but interaction-specific oscillation or conflict among autonomous work units is not demonstrated. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user permissions and request rate-limits bound individual requests; no negotiation or interference-specific response is supplied to two agents. - Decision owner: deterministic user/session isolation and rate limits, not an inter-agent coordination decision actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-user OpenShell backend, user-specific compiled graph/cache and session persistence. - Closure path: ordinary tasks remain isolated or rejected by policy; no cross-S1 interference signal is resolved and fed back to participating work units as coordination. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: multiple running model agents and isolation alone do not prove S2; optional DeepAgents subagents are default-disabled. - Evidence: per-user agent scope and subagent mode, chat entrypoints. - Basis: bounded negative; confidence: medium. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: a separately configured external organization could coordinate agents; it is outside this deployed app boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: current whole-platform supervision of user/session operations and shared action/tool entitlements, with administrator intervention. - Disturbance / variety regulated: aggregate activity, failure/error conditions, excessive or disallowed actions, competing user access to privileged tool capabilities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a legitimate administrator sees all-site recent per-turn metrics and cross-user activity and may disable a user, revise assigned roles or revise role-to-tool allocation for affected users. - Whole-system current view: administrator-only aggregated turn outcomes for all served users and accessible cross-user audit/users/traces, rather than just one model&#39;s chat narration. - Current-control decision scope: platform-wide role-to-tool entitlement revision and user-level disable/role adjustment, affecting future independently running agents rather than only a single current tool call. - Decision owner: authenticated human administrator (isadmin), not the ordinary agent, metrics service or RBAC cache. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: /api/metrics/summary global aggregates, cross-user user management, RBAC database, role-to-tool APIs, cache invalidation and AgentManager.createagent tool filtering. - Closure path: active multi-user tasks emit metric/audit status → admin reviews site-wide operating constraints → admin changes user or role tool entitlements → evictallagents or per-user eviction and RBAC refresh → subsequent user agent instances receive revised accessible tools. - Boundary reachability: all referenced APIs, current aggregates, authority checks and cache invalidation are wired into the shipped web application; this is an installed parent-operated mode, not a third-party dashboard or hypothetical policy hook. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: discretionary whole-current intervention belongs to the parent administrator; compiled agents merely obey changed operational permissions. This is not A or C for autonomous S3. - Evidence: site-wide summary, admin role tool intervention and invalidation, user role intervention, tool filtering on new agent. - Basis: structural first-party parent mode; confidence: medium. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: a human must actually interpret telemetry and choose constraints; aggregate metrics do not automatically allocate resources. This is primarily tool/permission control, not demonstrated dynamic budget or task-priority bargaining.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no sufficiently independent complementary verification and audit-judgment-to-control loop is shown beyond ordinary operational instrumentation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false-success assertions, unreported side effects and denied tool calls are risks; normal trace/audit tables collect execution events. - Decisive decision or feedback right: AuditService.record records write/exec or denied calls, and admin can query; no separately controlled raw-artifact, sampled replay or ground-truth audit verdict is demonstrated. - Decision owner: the same live runtime produces the events and records; human admin could use the logs but a supplied independent audit process is not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: paauditlog, patracelog, redacted event records and admin-only /api/audit endpoint. - Closure path: records can be inspected outside a conversation; none independently challenges production claims and returns a distinct audit finding to current control as shipped. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: dedicated table/endpoint names and detailed traces do not alone establish complementary audit independence. - Evidence: audit recording, query and filtering, trace collection, admin audit route. - Basis: bounded negative; confidence: medium. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: operational audit records can be valuable input to an externally operated auditor; they are not credited as an implemented independent judgment here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no demonstrated prospective external environmental inquiry, alternative adaptation selection and current-capability return loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new skills, changing user tasks and MCP/tool inventory may cause configuration changes, but the shipped app treats them as immediate user/admin actions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user can publish a skill and others can install it; administrator can upload skills. No shipped actor forecasts external changes and evaluates prospective options with S3. - Decision owner: external user/admin publishing, not an identifiable S4 adaptation decision actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: platformskill publish/add/upload routes, user skill packages and dynamic agent cache evictions. - Closure path: manual publishing can change subsequent tools, but not through an evidenced external-and-future options → S3 dialogue → selected capability adaptation loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: capability distribution and contextual memory do not by themselves establish prospective intelligence. - Evidence: platform skill publication and installation, agent skill wiring. - Basis: bounded negative; confidence: medium. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: a deployed organization might run an external skill roadmap, but this review cannot attribute it to the app.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no demonstrated identity-level/ultimate-purpose conflict escalation and legitimate policy decision return into the deployed system. - Disturbance / variety regulated: role violations, skill content restrictions, model prompt and tool-use security; these are operational restrictions rather than mission/identity tension. - Decisive decision or feedback right: admin owns local access changes; no first-party process receives ultimate policy disputes from S3/S4 and resolves them with governing authority. - Decision owner: operator/developer sets product mission outside this operational decision route. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: RBAC administration, fixed agent prompt, skill content checks and configuration. - Closure path: access and skill decisions change immediate permitted actions, but no identity/ultimate-policy matter returns as a changed governing mission or charter. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: operational parent S3 administration cannot be promoted to S5 merely because the same human is senior. - Evidence: RBAC administration, skill content checks, model configuration. - Basis: bounded negative; confidence: medium. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: external organizational governance cannot be inferred from admin menus and configuration.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/5045eb55011ef0e2da79b9b9fd4c4d3263b80d8f/assessments/cu/cuijiejie-open-agent-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 5045eb55011ef0e2da79b9b9fd4c4d3263b80d8f\">5045eb5 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T12:34:39+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Locotir/nexo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">nexo</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Locotir/nexo/tree/a9643683250aeff6050d0b77adfed0fe9392e53c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision a9643683250aeff6050d0b77adfed0fe9392e53c\">a964368 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Execute coding requests by repeatedly choosing tools, inspecting source/environment feedback, revising edits and returning an answer. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unfamiliar repository structure, failed commands/tests, permission denials, context-window pressure and changing task instructions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose the next coding/tool action or task subagent after inspecting the prior feedback and judge when the task response is complete. - Decision owner: Model-driven agent in the shipped runturn loop; the human supplies the task and approval boundary, while Rust handles transport and enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool registry, permission gate, max turns/cost, cancellation, context curator, sessions/checkpoints, loop/stale guards. - Closure path: User goal → model decision → first-party tool dispatch/permission → result or refusal appended to history → next model decision/final response. - Boundary reachability: turn::run constructs session, client, tools and optional SubAgent; runturn is directly used by the supported CLI/headless composition. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The substantive tool and code choices are returned by the model; the fixed gates do not select the edits. - Evidence: turn.rs#L165-L240, turn.rs#L1221-L1440, turn.rs#L1830-L1980. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high for the shipped decision/feedback loop. - Caveats: No paid-provider execution or benchmark success was independently reproduced in this review.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No first-party cross-S1 conflict-and-oscillation regulation is established beyond bounded delegation and tool safety. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent edits could interfere, but the shipped task design runs file-changing children in foreground and confines background children to read-only work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Detect an inter-S1 collision and choose a mutual adjustment returned into two operational cells; no such S2-specific decision path is shipped. - Decision owner: No qualifying S2 actor; deterministic subagent mode restrictions and the parent task selection manage task execution locally. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Read-only profile, foreground writing, task report, shared host permission gate and session job tracking. - Closure path: Child task → report → parent coding loop; no evidenced two-unit collision signal and negotiated/adjusted subsequent worker behaviour. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agent delegation and a serial-write safety rule are not the four-part S2 disturbance/attenuation/feedback witness. - Evidence: subagent.rs#L1-L96, subagent.rs#L174-L233, turn.rs#L165-L190. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high within the standard distribution. - Caveats: A user can construct a multi-session development team externally; that is not established by nexo&#39;s built-in task tool.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No separately established whole-system current control over a portfolio of operational units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Overspend, repetitive tool loops and stuck goal continuation inside one session. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Reallocate shared priorities/resources across distinct S1 units after a whole-current judgment; not supplied. - Decision owner: User chooses budget/permission limits, model chooses ordinary local work, deterministic runtime enforces stops. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session budget, max-turns, day cost, repeat/stale guards, job kill/status and permission modes. - Closure path: Guard stops or constrains the same coding turn; no whole-system allocation decision returns to multiple operational units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A cap or kill mechanism is enforcement, not an agent-owned S3 resource bargaining decision. - Evidence: turn.rs#L1221-L1277, turn.rs#L455-L527. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: The human user&#39;s wider coding organization was outside this session boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No sufficiently independent complementary audit of production claims with returned corrective control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: False coding completion claims or incorrect edits could require evidence outside the ordinary model/tool report. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Independently sample actual code/task reality, decide a challenge finding and cause control response; no built-in audit-specific right is supplied. - Decision owner: None established; model-selected explore children provide generic task evidence, user may inspect results. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Read-only subagent, ordinary tests, session/event history, diagnostics and CLI view. - Closure path: Child search/report returns to the same S1 model; not a separate audit judgment whose finding drives accountable correction. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An independent context or routine command/test does not by itself make a complementary S3 control channel. - Evidence: subagent.rs#L1-L96, turn.rs#L600-L620. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: A human could compose a separate reviewer workflow, but generic capability is not a shipped S3 path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No separate prospective environmental sensing and durable adaptation-option loop into future coding capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Model context pressure, stale file reads and changed task information are regulated as present coding context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Assess external/future changes and select a persistent change to harness capability, then return that option into present operations; not found. - Decision owner: Context selection is deterministic; agent makes current coding choices; model/provider configuration changes are user-owned. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Curator, reusable brief, goal continuation, model/provider configuration and session memory/recall. - Closure path: Selected history/notes enter the next current request, not an enduring outside-and-then adaptation proposal and current-capability revision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Context engineering and task planning do not establish prospective S4 capability governance. - Evidence: brief.rs#L1-L26, turn.rs#L685-L737, context.rs. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: A model may write future-facing plans as ordinary user-assigned work; they are not automatically harness-level S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party ultimate-policy dispute and authoritative identity-decision return loop at the selected coding session recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unsafe tools, untrusted repository agent files and permission escalation during local coding. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Decide an identity/ultimate-policy matter and return a binding revised policy to future operation; not supplied as a function-specific first-party path. - Decision owner: User/developer sets policy and explicitly approves individual actions; runtime enforces it without deciding the harness&#39;s identity. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Plan mode, trusted project filters, tool permission gate, prompt directives and persisted allow rules. - Closure path: User authorizes/refuses an individual tool and next tool action follows that result; no distinct identity-policy appeal → legitimate parent judgment → new binding organizational policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Permission policy and normal task approvals are not S5 governance by their existence. - Evidence: permission.rs#L1-L86, turn.rs#L165-L190, subagent.rs#L75-L118. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Maintainer governance of the Rust product is a different recursion; it cannot be imported as runtime S5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/bf3ab640c4094d36677b56db543f71f472f16f16/assessments/ne/nexo.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit bf3ab640c4094d36677b56db543f71f472f16f16\">bf3ab64 &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
